.PP
\f[V]-sO\f[R] can run together with a TCP, UDP or SCTP scan.
.SS FTP bounce scan (\f[V]-b <FTP relay host>\f[R])
.PP
Asks an FTP server (the relay) to open a data connection to each target
port, as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-b\f[R] does: after logging in, FeroProbe sends
one \f[V]PORT\f[R] command naming the target address and port, then
\f[V]LIST\f[R], over a single control connection.
The relay, not this machine, connects to the targets, so the result
shows what the targets look like from the relay\[aq]s network.
.PP
The argument is \f[V][user[:pass]\[at]]server[:port]\f[R]; the defaults
are user \f[V]anonymous\f[R], password \f[V]-wwwuser\[at]\f[R] and port
21, like nmap\[aq]s.
The last \f[V]\[at]\f[R] ends the login, so a password may contain
\f[V]\[at]\f[R].
Plain FTP sends the login in cleartext, so anyone on the path to the
relay can read a password given here; reports show the command line with
it masked (\f[V]user:****\[at]server\f[R]), so \f[V]--resume\f[R]
refuses such a report: run the bounce scan again instead.
.PP
.TS
