T}
.TE
.PP
After a reply that did not come in time, or an \f[V]ABOR\f[R], FeroProbe
logs in again before the next port, so a late reply cannot be read as
the answer about another port.
\f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] (and the timing templates\[aq] delay) waits
between ports.
.PP
If the relay refuses a \f[V]PORT\f[R] command for a port below 1024
before any privileged port went through, FeroProbe warns and skips the
privileged ports, as nmap does; if it refuses an unprivileged one, the
relay cannot bounce at all and the scan stops with an error.
The reason shown with \f[V]--reason\f[R] is \f[V]ftp-bounce\f[R] (or
\f[V]no-response\f[R] for \f[V]filtered\f[R]).
.PP
\f[V]-b\f[R] is a TCP scan of its own: it replaces the default TCP scan
and cannot be combined with \f[V]-sS\f[R], \f[V]-sT\f[R] or the other
TCP scans.
It is IPv4 only, because \f[V]PORT\f[R] carries an IPv4 address, and it
needs no privileges.
Host discovery still runs from this machine, so FeroProbe prints
nmap\[aq]s hint to add \f[V]-Pn\f[R] when the targets are only reachable
from the relay.
The FTP commands and answers show in \f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] as
\f[V]SENT ... FTP\f[R] and \f[V]RCVD ... FTP\f[R] lines (the password as
\f[V]****\f[R]), and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] lists one line per port.
.PP
Like the evasion options, the bounce scan is refused when any target or
the relay has a public address, unless \f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R]
is given; see the authorization model.
Few FTP servers still allow it (RFC 2577 asks them not to).
.SS Version detection (\f[V]-sV\f[R])
.PP
Listed with the scan types because it is given as \f[V]-s\f[R] option;
see Service and version detection.
.SH PORT SPECIFICATION AND SCAN ORDER
.PP
Without \f[V]-p\f[R], \f[V]-F\f[R] or \f[V]--top-ports\f[R], FeroProbe
scans the 1000 most common TCP ports and the 1000 most common UDP ports,
as nmap does, the 50 most common SCTP ports, and, with \f[V]-sO\f[R],
all 256 IP protocols.
.PP
The TCP and UDP lists are nmap\[aq]s: the same ports in the same order
as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]--top-ports\f[R], ranked by the frequencies in
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]nmap-services\f[R] (nmap 7.94).
The default scan, \f[V]-F\f[R] (the first 100) and
\f[V]--top-ports N\f[R] (the first \f[V]N\f[R]) cover the same ports as
nmap\[aq]s.
Only the port numbers and their order are taken; the names FeroProbe
prints come from the IANA registry.
.PP
\f[V]-p-\f[R] scans all 65535.
.SS \f[V]-p <port ranges>\f[R], \f[V]--ports <port ranges>\f[R]
.PP
Scan only these ports.
A comma-separated list of single ports (\f[V]22\f[R]), ranges
(\f[V]1-1024\f[R]), open-ended ranges (\f[V]-1024\f[R],
\f[V]60000-\f[R]) and \f[V]-\f[R] for all of 1-65535 (\f[V]-p-\f[R]).
Port 0 cannot be scanned.
A protocol prefix applies to the ports after it, up to the next prefix:
.PP
.TS
