Only read replies to this probe
T}
T{
\f[V]product\f[R], \f[V]version\f[R]
T}@T{
The product and version; \f[V]${1}\f[R], \f[V]${2}\f[R], ...
are the regex\[aq]s groups
T}
T{
\f[V]info TEXT\f[R]
T}@T{
Extra information; may be repeated, joined with \f[V]; \f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]host\f[R], \f[V]os\f[R], \f[V]device\f[R]
T}@T{
What goes in the \f[V]Service Info\f[R] line
T}
T{
\f[V]cpe TEXT\f[R]
T}@T{
A CPE name such as \f[V]cpe:/a:openbsd:openssh:${2}\f[R]; may be
repeated
T}
.TE
.SH OS DETECTION
.SS \f[V]-O\f[R]
.PP
Enable OS detection.
For every host where the port scan found an open TCP port, FeroProbe
sends a small set of probes, reads how the host\[aq]s TCP/IP stack
answers, and matches that fingerprint against its own OS database, which
names OS versions where the probes can tell them apart (for example
Linux 4.19 - 6.x against Linux 3.x - 4.18, or Windows 10 - 11 against
Windows 7).
.PP
One try sends a host at most 9 probes, all to ports and protocols the
command already probes:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Probe
T}@T{
Sent to
T}@T{
What it is
T}
_
T{
\f[V]s1\f[R]-\f[V]s4\f[R]
T}@T{
the open TCP port
T}@T{
four SYNs with different TCP option sets, 100 ms apart
T}
T{
\f[V]e\f[R]
T}@T{
the open TCP port
T}@T{
a SYN with ECE and CWR (asks for ECN)
T}
T{
\f[V]a\f[R]
T}@T{
the open TCP port
T}@T{
an ACK outside any connection
T}
T{
\f[V]c1\f[R], \f[V]c2\f[R]
T}@T{
a closed TCP port, if the scan found one
T}@T{
a SYN and an ACK
T}
T{
\f[V]ie\f[R]
T}@T{
the host, only if the command\[aq]s host discovery sends ICMP echo
requests (the default as root, or \f[V]-PE\f[R])
T}@T{
an ICMP or ICMPv6 echo request with the \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] payload
T}
.TE
.PP
The fingerprint records, per probe, whether it was answered, the TCP
flags, window, option layout and \[dq]don\[aq]t fragment\[dq] bit of the
answer, and across probes the initial TTL (IPv6: hop limit), the window
scale, the IP ID sequence (IPv4), how the TCP timestamp clock runs, and
whether the host answers ECN.
Each signature in the database lists the values it expects; its accuracy
is the share of its tests (weighted) that match.
A signature that matches every test the fingerprint has is a perfect
match, reported as the OS:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Device type: general purpose
Running: Linux 4.X|5.X|6.X
OS CPE: cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:4 cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:5 cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:6
OS details: Linux 4.19 - 6.x
Network Distance: 1 hop
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Without a perfect match FeroProbe prints
\f[V]No exact OS matches for host\f[R] and the fingerprint (lines
starting with \f[V]OS:\f[R]), so it can be added to the database, see
docs/os-fingerprints.md.
Signatures that match at 85% or better are shown as
\f[V]Aggressive OS guesses\f[R] with \f[V]--osscan-guess\f[R], or when
the conditions were not ideal (for example no closed port), as in nmap.
With \f[V]-v\f[R] the IP ID sequence class is shown
(\f[V]IP ID Sequence Generation: All zeros\f[R]); with \f[V]-vv\f[R] the
fingerprint is shown even for a perfect match.
The network distance is 0 for this machine, 1 for a host whose MAC
address discovery found, and otherwise counted from the TTL.
.PP
As in nmap, a host without a perfect match is tried again, up to
\f[V]--max-os-tries\f[R] times (default 5), or at most twice when the
conditions make a match unlikely: no closed TCP port, a host more than
five hops away, or a \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] above 500 ms.
A host with an open but no closed TCP port gets the warning
\f[V]OSScan results may be unreliable because we could not find at least 1 open and 1 closed port\f[R].
A host without an open TCP port gets no OS probes at all (nmap would
probe its closed port and random closed ports outside the scan, see
Differences from nmap).
.PP
The XML report has nmap\[aq]s \f[V]<os>\f[R] element
(\f[V]<portused>\f[R], \f[V]<osmatch>\f[R] with \f[V]<osclass>\f[R] and
\f[V]<cpe>\f[R], \f[V]<osfingerprint>\f[R]), then \f[V]<distance>\f[R]
and \f[V]<ipidsequence>\f[R]; the grepable report lists perfect matches
after \f[V]OS:\f[R]; the JSON report has the full result under
\f[V]os\f[R].
.PP
\f[V]-O\f[R] needs raw sockets (Linux, root or \f[V]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R])
and a port scan; it is an error without raw sockets and cannot be
combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
If no port scan is left to run (every scan type lost its ports), OS
detection is skipped with a warning.
IPv4 and IPv6 hosts are both covered; the answers are read from a packet
capture, which shows the IP header of both.
The TCP probes deliberately carry no FeroProbe window marker, since they
must look like a real client\[aq]s SYNs; the echo request carries the
usual \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] payload.
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] show each probe\[aq]s
TCP options (\f[V]<mss 1460,sackOK,timestamp ...>\f[R]).
With \f[V]ics_protection\f[R] on, \f[V]-O\f[R] is refused (see ICS
protection).
.SS \f[V]--osscan-limit\f[R]
.PP
Only run OS detection against hosts where the scan found at least one
open and one closed (or unfiltered) TCP port, as in nmap.
Other hosts are skipped (with \f[V]-v\f[R], a line says so), which saves
time on many hosts.
No effect without \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R] (a warning says so).
.SS \f[V]--osscan-guess\f[R], \f[V]--fuzzy\f[R]
.PP
When no signature matches perfectly, also print the near matches (85% or
better) as \f[V]Aggressive OS guesses: Linux 4.19 - 6.x (93%), ...\f[R]
and the \f[V]Running (JUST GUESSING)\f[R] line, as in nmap.
No effect without \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--max-os-tries <tries>\f[R]
.PP
How many tries a host without a perfect match gets (default 5, at least
1).
Each try sends the probe set again.
A lower value is faster and gentler on hosts the database does not know;
it does not raise the limit of two tries for hosts without a closed
port.
No effect without \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]-A\f[R].
.SH TRACEROUTE AND AGGRESSIVE MODE
.SS \f[V]--traceroute\f[R]
.PP
After the port scan, trace the route to each host that is up.
FeroProbe sends TCP SYN probes with TTL (IPv6: hop limit) 1 to 30 all at
once, to a TCP port the scan found open on that host, else one it found
closed, else a fallback port the command names: the first port of the
TCP scan; with \f[V]-sn\f[R], the first TCP port given with
\f[V]-p\f[R]; else the first TCP port of host discovery (\f[V]-PS\f[R],
\f[V]-PA\f[R], or the default 443 as root).
Traceroute never probes a port outside the scan: when the command probes
no TCP port at all (for example \f[V]-sU -Pn\f[R]), hosts without an
open or closed TCP port are not traced and a warning says why (with
\f[V]-sn\f[R], name the port with \f[V]-p\f[R]).
A directly connected host needs no port, since it gets no probes (see
below).
Routers answer the probes that expire with ICMP time exceeded, and the
first probe that reaches the host ends the route.
A hop that never answers is shown as \f[V]...\f[R].
Needs raw sockets.
With \f[V]-Pn\f[R] every target is traced, even one that never answered.
Hosts given up on by \f[V]--host-timeout\f[R] are not traced.
.PP
As in nmap, this machine itself (a loopback address or one of its own)
is not traced at all, and a host on a directly connected network gets no
probes: its route is the host alone, at hop 1, with the latency the scan
measured, under a plain \f[V]TRACEROUTE\f[R] header (in XML, a
\f[V]<trace>\f[R] element without a port).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
TRACEROUTE (using port 443/tcp)
HOP RTT        ADDRESS
1   0.29 ms    192.168.1.1
2   ...
3   9.84 ms    203.0.113.5
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To trace without a port scan, combine it with \f[V]-sn\f[R], and with
\f[V]-sn -Pn\f[R] to skip host discovery too:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
sudo feroprobe -sn --traceroute example.org
sudo feroprobe -sn -Pn --traceroute -p 443 example.org
\f[R]
.fi
.SS \f[V]-A\f[R] (aggressive detection)
.PP
The same as \f[V]-sV -O -sC --traceroute\f[R], as in nmap: version
detection, OS detection, the default scripts (see \f[V]-sC\f[R]) and
traceroute.
Without raw sockets only \f[V]-sV\f[R] and \f[V]-sC\f[R] run, rather
than failing, as in nmap.
Unlike nmap, which drops the other parts silently, FeroProbe prints a
warning naming them:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Warning: -A without root or CAP_NET_RAW skips OS detection (-O) and traceroute (--traceroute), which send raw packets; only version detection (-sV) and the default scripts (-sC) run. Run as root, or grant CAP_NET_RAW, to include them
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[V]--script\f[R] given with \f[V]-A\f[R] adds to the default scripts.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R] or \f[V]-sL\f[R].
With ICS protection on, \f[V]-A\f[R] is refused as a whole.
.SH SCRIPT SCANNING
.PP
After the scan, FeroProbe can run scripts against the hosts and ports it
found, like nmap\[aq]s scripting engine.
Scripts are written in Lua 5.4, which is built into FeroProbe.
Each script decides from what the scan found whether it runs on a host
or a port (its \f[I]rule\f[R]), then its \f[I]action\f[R] talks to the
service and returns a result, which the report shows under the port or
the host.
.PP
The scripts are FeroProbe\[aq]s own, with FeroProbe\[aq]s own script
library (\f[V]fero\f[R]).
nmap\[aq]s scripts do not run unchanged: they are built on nmap\[aq]s
libraries (\f[V]stdnse\f[R], \f[V]shortport\f[R],
\f[V]nmap.new_socket\f[R] and the rest), which FeroProbe does not have
and cannot ship under their licence.
FeroProbe ships its own scripts, written from the protocols\[aq]
specifications (see Built-in scripts): \f[V]-sC\f[R] runs the ones in
the \f[V]default\f[R] category, and more come in later releases.
You can run your own: see Writing scripts.
.PP
Scripts run in a sandbox and can only reach the host they run against,
and only the ports the scan probed there: see Script sandbox and scope.
.PP
Scripts run last for each host group, after version and OS detection and
traceroute, on the hosts found up (every host with \f[V]-Pn\f[R]) that
were not given up on by \f[V]--host-timeout\f[R], as in nmap.
Scripts with a \f[V]prerule\f[R] run once before host discovery, and
scripts with a \f[V]postrule\f[R] once after the last host.
With \f[V]-sn\f[R], scripts with a \f[V]hostrule\f[R] still run, but no
port is probed, so they can open no socket.
A list scan (\f[V]-sL\f[R]) refuses \f[V]--script\f[R] and
\f[V]-sC\f[R], since it sends the targets nothing.
With ICS protection on, \f[V]--script\f[R], \f[V]-sC\f[R] and
\f[V]-A\f[R] are refused (see Settings files and ICS protection).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ feroprobe -p 22,25 --script banner 192.0.2.10
PORT       STATE          SERVICE
22/tcp     open           ssh
|_banner: SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6
25/tcp     open           smtp
|_banner: 220 mail.example ESMTP Postfix
\f[R]
.fi
.SS \f[V]-sC\f[R] (default scripts)
.PP
Run the scripts in the \f[V]default\f[R] category, the same as
\f[V]--script default\f[R], as in nmap.
\f[V]-A\f[R] includes it.
Given with \f[V]--script\f[R], the default scripts run as well as the
ones \f[V]--script\f[R] names.
Like nmap\[aq]s default set, these scripts are chosen to be quick, to
say something useful about common services, and to be safe: they read
greetings, capabilities and keys, never try passwords other than an
anonymous login where the protocol defines one (FTP), and change nothing
on the target.
They run on a port only when the port is open and its number or service
(from the port number, or from \f[V]-sV\f[R]) is theirs; see Built-in
scripts for the list.
As every script, they reach only the ports the scan probed:
\f[V]ftp-anon\f[R] therefore lists no files (a listing would come over a
second connection to a port the server picks), and nothing is sent to a
port outside \f[V]-p\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ feroprobe -sCV -p 21,22,80 192.0.2.10
PORT       STATE          SERVICE        VERSION
21/tcp     open           ftp            vsftpd 3.0.5
|_ftp-anon: Anonymous FTP login allowed (FTP code 230)
22/tcp     open           ssh            OpenSSH 9.6p1 Ubuntu 3ubuntu13
| ssh-hostkey: 
|   256 4f:0a:...:91 (ECDSA)
|_  256 c2:7e:...:3b (ED25519)
80/tcp     open           http           nginx 1.24.0
| http-methods: 
|_  Supported Methods: GET HEAD POST OPTIONS
| http-robots.txt: 1 disallowed entry
|_/admin
|_http-title: Welcome
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
With ICS protection on, \f[V]-sC\f[R] is refused like
\f[V]--script\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--script <scripts>\f[R]
.PP
Choose the scripts to run: a comma-separated list where each item is
.IP \[bu] 2
a script name, such as \f[V]banner\f[R], with \f[V]*\f[R] matching any
run of characters (\f[V]http-*\f[R]); a trailing \f[V].lua\f[R] is
allowed;
.IP \[bu] 2
a category, such as \f[V]safe\f[R] or \f[V]discovery\f[R], or
\f[V]all\f[R] for every script;
.IP \[bu] 2
a boolean expression of names and categories with \f[V]and\f[R],
\f[V]or\f[R], \f[V]not\f[R] and parentheses, such as
\f[V]\[dq]safe and not intrusive\[dq]\f[R].
As in nmap, \f[V]and\f[R] and \f[V]or\f[R] bind equally and group from
the right (\f[V]a and b or c\f[R] is \f[V]a and (b or c)\f[R]), so use
parentheses when mixing them;
.IP \[bu] 2
a script file, given by its path, with or without \f[V].lua\f[R];
.IP \[bu] 2
a directory, given by its path with a trailing \f[V]/\f[R], for every
\f[V].lua\f[R] file in it.
Without the slash it is refused, as in nmap, so that a directory named
like a category is never picked by mistake.
.PP
Names, categories and expressions choose among the scripts built into
FeroProbe and the scripts installed in your scripts directory that its
index lists.
An item that matches none of them is tried as a file, then as a
directory, then as the name of a script in the scripts directory
(\f[V]--script my-check\f[R] finds \f[V]my-check.lua\f[R] there even
before it is indexed, as nmap does); one that matches nothing at all
stops the run before anything is sent, as does a script that fails to
load, or two scripts with the same name.
A \f[V]+\f[R] before an item runs its scripts on every host and port
whatever their rules say (scripts with a \f[V]portrule\f[R] still only
see the ports that may be open).
\f[V]--script\f[R] may be given more than once; the lists add up.
\f[V]-v\f[R] prints how many scripts were loaded.
.PP
Each script is loaded (its file run in the sandbox to read its fields)
when FeroProbe starts.
Its rules are checked in one sandbox per host (the hostrule and the
portrules of that host\[aq]s ports), and its action runs in a fresh
sandbox for every host or port, so actions share nothing with each
other, with the rules or between runs.
.SS \f[V]--script-args <args>\f[R]
.PP
Arguments for the scripts, in nmap\[aq]s syntax: comma-separated
\f[V]name=value\f[R] pairs and bare names (flags), where a value is a
word, a quoted string (\f[V]\[dq]a,b\[dq]\f[R] or
\f[V]\[aq]a,b\[aq]\f[R], with \f[V]\[rs]\f[R] escaping the next
character) or a \f[V]{...}\f[R] table of the same:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe --script banner --script-args \[aq]banner.timeout=2000,banner.length=80\[aq] 192.0.2.10
feroprobe --script mine.lua --script-args \[aq]paths={/,/admin},user=admin,unsafe\[aq] 192.0.2.10
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Names are usually prefixed with the script\[aq]s name, as above.
A later name replaces an earlier one.
Arguments that do not parse stop the run.
A script reads them with \f[V]fero.args.get\f[R] (see Writing scripts).
.SS \f[V]--script-args-file <file>\f[R]
.PP
Read script arguments from a file, in the same syntax, where newlines
separate arguments as commas do.
Trailing commas are ignored.
Arguments given with \f[V]--script-args\f[R] are added after the
file\[aq]s, so they win.
.SS \f[V]--script-help <scripts>\f[R]
.PP
Print each chosen script\[aq]s name, categories, origin (built into
FeroProbe, or the file it came from) and description, then stop.
\f[V]<scripts>\f[R] is read as for \f[V]--script\f[R].
It needs no target, sends nothing and is never refused by ICS
protection.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ feroprobe --script-help banner

banner
Categories: discovery safe
Built into FeroProbe
  Shows the first line of text a TCP service sends when a connection
  opens, ...
\f[R]
.fi
.SS \f[V]--script-trace\f[R]
.PP
Print everything the scripts\[aq] connections do on standard output, one
line each, in nmap\[aq]s script-trace layout: the protocol, the local
and remote ends, \f[V]>\f[R] towards the target or \f[V]<\f[R] from it,
and after the \f[V]|\f[R] either an event (\f[V]CONNECT\f[R],
\f[V]CLOSE\f[R], \f[V]EOF\f[R], \f[V]TIMEOUT\f[R] or the error) or the
data sent or received.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
LUA: TCP 192.0.2.1:40312 > 192.0.2.10:21 | CONNECT
LUA: TCP 192.0.2.1:40312 < 192.0.2.10:21 | 220 (vsFTPd 3.0.5)\[rs]r\[rs]n
LUA: TCP 192.0.2.1:40312 > 192.0.2.10:21 | USER anonymous\[rs]r\[rs]n
LUA: TCP 192.0.2.1:40312 > 192.0.2.10:21 | CLOSE
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Data that is mostly text (at most 5% of its bytes unprintable, as nmap
decides) is shown on one line, with line ends and tabs as
\f[V]\[rs]r\f[R], \f[V]\[rs]n\f[R], \f[V]\[rs]t\f[R], a backslash as
\f[V]\[rs]\[rs]\f[R] and other bytes as \f[V]\[rs]xHH\f[R].
Other data is shown as a hex dump, sixteen bytes a line with their
printable characters.
Connections that speak TLS show the data inside the TLS, as nmap\[aq]s
do.
The HTTP library\[aq]s connections are traced too.
The prefix is \f[V]LUA:\f[R] (nmap\[aq]s is \f[V]NSE:\f[R], the name of
its engine); the rest of the line is in nmap\[aq]s layout.
.PP
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] turns the script trace on as well, as in nmap
(and the \f[V]p\f[R] key while a scan runs turns both on and off).
Without \f[V]--script\f[R] or \f[V]-sC\f[R] the option does nothing, and
a warning says so.
.SS \f[V]--script-updatedb\f[R]
.PP
Index the scripts in your scripts directory by category, then stop.
It needs no target and sends nothing.
.PP
The scripts directory is \f[V]$XDG_DATA_HOME/feroprobe/scripts\f[R],
which is \f[V]\[ti]/.local/share/feroprobe/scripts\f[R] when
\f[V]XDG_DATA_HOME\f[R] is not set (on Windows,
\f[V]%APPDATA%\[rs]feroprobe\[rs]scripts\f[R]).
Put your own \f[V].lua\f[R] scripts there, then run:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ feroprobe --script-updatedb
Updating the script database in /home/me/.local/share/feroprobe/scripts.
Script database updated: 2 scripts.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each script is loaded in the sandbox (as when it runs, with no network)
to read its categories, and the result is written to \f[V]script.db\f[R]
in that directory, one line per script, in nmap\[aq]s layout:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Entry { filename = \[dq]my-check.lua\[dq], categories = { \[dq]discovery\[dq], \[dq]safe\[dq], } }
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
From then on \f[V]--script\f[R] and \f[V]-sC\f[R] choose those scripts
by category and expression as well as by name: a script of yours in
\f[V]default\f[R] runs with \f[V]-sC\f[R], as in nmap.
A script added or whose categories changed is chosen by category only
after the next \f[V]--script-updatedb\f[R]; by name it is found at once.
A script that does not load, or that has a built-in script\[aq]s name,
is left out of the index with a warning (give its path to
\f[V]--script\f[R] to run it anyway).
A \f[V]script.db\f[R] that FeroProbe cannot read stops the run with a
message naming the fix, and one that lists a script that has gone does
too.
FeroProbe reads the index as data and never runs it.
.PP
Under \f[V]sudo\f[R], FeroProbe gives up root for the user who ran
\f[V]sudo\f[R] (see Privileges and platforms), and uses that user\[aq]s
scripts directory, so \f[V]sudo feroprobe --script-updatedb\f[R] and
\f[V]sudo feroprobe -sC ...\f[R] see your own scripts.
With \f[V]--keep-root\f[R], or as root without \f[V]sudo\f[R], it is
root\[aq]s.
nmap uses the \f[V]\[ti]/.nmap\f[R] of whoever it runs as.
.SS \f[V]--script-timeout <time>\f[R]
.PP
The longest one script may run against one host or port, counted from
its start.
A script that runs longer is stopped, and gives no output, as in nmap;
\f[V]-d\f[R] names it.
\f[V]0\f[R] means no limit.
By default the limit is ten minutes at every timing.
nmap has none by default and sets ten minutes only with \f[V]-T5\f[R];
FeroProbe always sets one, so a server that answers very slowly (a byte
at a time, say) cannot hold a script for hours.
.SS Script sandbox and scope
.PP
Every script runs in its own Lua state with:
.IP \[bu] 2
Lua\[aq]s \f[V]string\f[R], \f[V]table\f[R], \f[V]math\f[R],
\f[V]utf8\f[R] and \f[V]coroutine\f[R] libraries, the basic functions,
\f[V]os.time\f[R], \f[V]os.clock\f[R], \f[V]os.date\f[R] and
\f[V]os.difftime\f[R], and FeroProbe\[aq]s \f[V]fero\f[R] library;
.IP \[bu] 2
no \f[V]io\f[R], no other \f[V]os\f[R] functions (no commands,
environment, files or exit), no \f[V]require\f[R], \f[V]package\f[R],
\f[V]debug\f[R], \f[V]dofile\f[R], \f[V]loadfile\f[R] or
\f[V]collectgarbage\f[R], no \f[V]string.dump\f[R], and \f[V]load\f[R]
for source text only, never precompiled bytecode;
.IP \[bu] 2
64 MB of memory, and about a billion Lua instructions (some seconds of
pure computation; time spent waiting on the network does not count);
.IP \[bu] 2
for rules, ten seconds (or \f[V]--script-timeout\f[R], if shorter); for
loading a script, two seconds;
.IP \[bu] 2
at most 32 sockets open at once.
.PP
When a limit is reached the script is stopped, and \f[V]pcall\f[R],
\f[V]xpcall\f[R] and \f[V]coroutine.resume\f[R] pass that error on
rather than catching it, so a script cannot carry on past it.
.PP
Lua\[aq]s pattern functions (\f[V]string.find\f[R],
\f[V]string.match\f[R], \f[V]string.gmatch\f[R] and
\f[V]string.gsub\f[R]) are FeroProbe\[aq]s own, not Lua\[aq]s: Lua runs
each of them as a single step that no limit can interrupt, so a pattern
that backtracks heavily over a long text could hold a script far past
its timeout.
FeroProbe\[aq]s versions count every matching step against the
instruction limit and check the deadline as they go, and the result of
\f[V]string.gsub\f[R] may grow no larger than the memory limit.
They take the same arguments, give the same results and raise the same
errors as Lua 5.4\[aq]s, with the same limits (32 captures, nesting 200
deep).
.PP
\f[B]Scope.\f[R] The engine checks every connection, rather than
trusting the script.
A script running against a host may connect only to that host (its
address, the name it was given on the command line or its reverse DNS
name; no name is looked up) and only to a port of the same protocol that
the scan probed on it, in any state.
Only TCP sockets (plain or TLS) and UDP sockets exist.
The HTTP library goes through the same check, and follows a redirect
only when it points inside the scope; one that points elsewhere is not
followed and is returned to the script as it came.
Scripts with a \f[V]prerule\f[R] or \f[V]postrule\f[R], which run
without a host, have no network access at all, so nothing broadcasts.
A connection the script asks for outside the scope fails, and FeroProbe
prints a warning naming the script and the address.
A wider scope may come later as an explicit option.
.SS Writing scripts
.PP
A script is a \f[V].lua\f[R] file that sets these globals (the names are
nmap\[aq]s, so the shape is familiar):
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
