.PP
\f[B]Output.\f[R] \f[V]action\f[R] returns a string, which is the output
as it is; a table, which the report lays out as indented lines (its
numbered entries first, as their value alone, then its named entries in
name order, as \f[V]name: value\f[R]) and which XML and JSON keep as
structured data; or a table and a string, the string being the text
shown.
Returning nothing gives no output.
An error in a rule or action gives no output either; \f[V]-d\f[R] shows
it.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
description = [[Says whether a TCP service greets first.]]
categories = {\[dq]safe\[dq]}

portrule = function(host, port)
  return port.protocol == \[dq]tcp\[dq] and port.state == \[dq]open\[dq]
end

action = function(host, port)
  local sock = fero.socket.new()
  sock:set_timeout(tonumber(fero.args.get(SCRIPT_NAME .. \[dq].timeout\[dq])) or 3000)
  if not sock:connect(host, port) then return nil end
  local ok, data = sock:receive()
  sock:close()
  return { greets = tostring(ok), bytes = ok and #data or 0 }
end
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Script TLS and HTTP
.PP
\f[B]TLS.\f[R] Script TLS (\f[V]\[dq]ssl\[dq]\f[R] sockets,
\f[V]reconnect_ssl\f[R], the HTTP library) speaks TLS 1.2 and 1.3.
It accepts any certificate without checking it, offers no client
certificate, and sends the host\[aq]s name as SNI when it has one (the
name given on the command line, else its reverse DNS name; never an
address).
nmap, through OpenSSL, can also speak older versions, so a device that
only knows SSLv3 to TLS 1.1 answers nmap\[aq]s scripts but not
FeroProbe\[aq]s.
.PP
\f[V]sock:get_ssl_certificate()\f[R] returns \f[V]subject\f[R] and
\f[V]issuer\f[R] (tables of attribute names such as
\f[V]commonName\f[R], \f[V]organizationName\f[R] and
\f[V]countryName\f[R] to their values), \f[V]subject_text\f[R] and
\f[V]issuer_text\f[R] (every attribute in the certificate\[aq]s order,
as \f[V]name=value/name=value\f[R]), \f[V]serial\f[R] (hex),
\f[V]version\f[R], \f[V]sig_algorithm\f[R] (such as
\f[V]sha256WithRSAEncryption\f[R]), \f[V]pubkey\f[R] (\f[V]type\f[R]:
\f[V]rsa\f[R], \f[V]ec\f[R], \f[V]dsa\f[R], \f[V]ed25519\f[R]...;
\f[V]bits\f[R]; \f[V]curve\f[R] for EC keys), \f[V]validity\f[R]
(\f[V]notBefore\f[R] and \f[V]notAfter\f[R], as
\f[V]YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS\f[R] in UTC), \f[V]san\f[R] (the subject
alternative names, as \f[V]DNS:name\f[R], \f[V]IP:address\f[R],
\f[V]email:...\f[R], \f[V]URI:...\f[R]), \f[V]sha1\f[R] and
\f[V]sha256\f[R] (fingerprints in hex), \f[V]pem\f[R] and \f[V]der\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]HTTP.\f[R] \f[V]fero.http\f[R] speaks HTTP/1.1, one request per
connection (\f[V]Connection: close\f[R]), and sends a \f[V]Host\f[R]
header (the host\[aq]s name, else its address, with the port unless it
is 80, or 443 over TLS) and a \f[V]User-Agent\f[R] that names FeroProbe:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; FeroProbe Scripting Engine; https://github.com/jkobierczynski/FeroProbe)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This is the script engine\[aq]s signature (see
\f[V]docs/responsible-use.md\f[R]).
A request goes over TLS or not as the port suggests: TLS when
\f[V]-sV\f[R] found it, or the service name or port number is a TLS one;
if that attempt gets no HTTP answer, the other is tried, as nmap does.
A request carrying credentials (\f[V]Authorization\f[R],
\f[V]Proxy-Authorization\f[R] or \f[V]Cookie\f[R]) that started over TLS
never falls back to plain text, and a redirect that leaves TLS for plain
text, or goes to another port, drops those headers.
The options table takes:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Option
T}@T{
T}
_
T{
\f[V]header\f[R]
T}@T{
A table of extra headers, name to value; a \f[V]Host\f[R] or
\f[V]User-Agent\f[R] here replaces FeroProbe\[aq]s
T}
T{
\f[V]content\f[R]
T}@T{
The request body
T}
T{
\f[V]timeout\f[R]
T}@T{
Milliseconds for the whole request, both attempts included; a first
attempt gets at most half while a second may follow (default 30000)
T}
T{
\f[V]ssl\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]true\f[R] or \f[V]false\f[R] to use TLS or not, with no second
attempt
T}
T{
\f[V]redirect_ok\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]false\f[R] to follow no redirect, or how many to follow (default 3)
T}
T{
\f[V]max_body_size\f[R]
T}@T{
The most body bytes kept (default 2 MiB, at most 16 MiB)
T}
T{
\f[V]truncated_ok\f[R]
T}@T{
Return a body cut at \f[V]max_body_size\f[R]; without it a longer body
