T}
T{
\f[V]auth\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]{username = ..., password = ...}\f[R] for basic authentication
T}
T{
\f[V]cookies\f[R]
T}@T{
A \f[V]Cookie\f[R] header, as a string or a table of names to values
T}
T{
\f[V]user_agent\f[R]
T}@T{
Another \f[V]User-Agent\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
The response table has \f[V]status\f[R] (a number),
\f[V]status-line\f[R], \f[V]version\f[R] (\f[V]\[dq]1.1\[dq]\f[R]),
\f[V]header\f[R] (names in lower case; a repeated header\[aq]s values
joined with \f[V], \f[R]), \f[V]rawheader\f[R] (every header line as
sent), \f[V]cookies\f[R] (one table per \f[V]Set-Cookie\f[R], with
\f[V]name\f[R], \f[V]value\f[R] and its attributes in lower case, flags
such as \f[V]httponly\f[R] as \f[V]true\f[R]), \f[V]body\f[R],
\f[V]ssl\f[R] (whether TLS was used), \f[V]truncated\f[R], and
\f[V]location\f[R] (the URLs of the redirects followed, in order; the
response is the last one\[aq]s).
A request that fails returns \f[V]nil\f[R] and a message, such as
\f[V]\[dq]TIMEOUT\[dq]\f[R] or a scope refusal; in nmap the response
table comes back with no \f[V]status\f[R].
Bodies are not decompressed; FeroProbe sends no
\f[V]Accept-Encoding\f[R], so servers send them plain.
A method, path or header value holding a control character (such as CR
or LF) is refused.
.PP
Script arguments that apply to every HTTP request, as in nmap:
\f[V]http.useragent\f[R] (another \f[V]User-Agent\f[R]),
\f[V]http.host\f[R] (another \f[V]Host\f[R] header),
\f[V]http.max-body-size\f[R] and \f[V]http.truncated-ok\f[R].
A \f[V]User-Agent\f[R] set this way, or by the script, replaces the
FeroProbe signature: like \f[V]--data\f[R], it is a deliberate choice,
and the request carries exactly what was asked for.
.SS Script output
.PP
In the normal report each script\[aq]s output follows its port, or comes
under \f[V]Host script results:\f[R] after the host\[aq]s other results,
\f[V]Pre-scan script results:\f[R] before the first host and
\f[V]Post-scan script results:\f[R] after the last, in nmap\[aq]s
layout: \f[V]|_name: output\f[R] for one line, and \f[V]| \f[R] before
every line but the last for more.
Control characters other than tab and newline are shown as
\f[V]\[rs]xHH\f[R], so a service cannot send terminal escape sequences
through a script.
Results are listed by script name.
In the names of a returned table, a backslash is doubled, so a name
holding the byte 0xFF (\f[V]\[rs]xFF\f[R]) stays apart from one holding
the text \f[V]\[rs]xFF\f[R] (\f[V]\[rs]\[rs]xFF\f[R]).
.PP
The XML report has nmap\[aq]s elements:
\f[V]<script id=\[dq]...\[dq] output=\[dq]...\[dq]>\f[R] in
\f[V]<port>\f[R], \f[V]<hostscript>\f[R] in \f[V]<host>\f[R], and
\f[V]<prescript>\f[R] and \f[V]<postscript>\f[R] in \f[V]<nmaprun>\f[R],
with a returned table as nested \f[V]<elem key=\[dq]...\[dq]>\f[R] and
\f[V]<table key=\[dq]...\[dq]>\f[R] elements.
The JSON report has a \f[V]scripts\f[R] list in each port and host, and
\f[V]prescript\f[R] and \f[V]postscript\f[R] lists at the top, each
entry with \f[V]id\f[R], \f[V]output\f[R] and, for a table,
\f[V]data\f[R] (an array for a table with only numbered entries, else an
object with the numbered entries under \f[V]\[dq]1\[dq]\f[R],
\f[V]\[dq]2\[dq]\f[R], ....
Lua lets \f[V]t[\[dq]1\[dq]]\f[R] and \f[V]t[1]\f[R] both exist, so a
name made only of digits and not starting with \f[V]0\f[R], or a name
that starts with a double quote, is written in an added pair of double
quotes: \f[V]t[\[dq]1\[dq]]\f[R] as
\f[V]\[dq]\[rs]\[dq]1\[rs]\[dq]\[dq]\f[R].
A bare whole number is then always a numbered entry, a quoted key a name
with the outer quotes removed, and any other key a name as it is).
At most 100,000 values and 16 MiB of text are kept from one result; the
rest is shown as \f[V]...\f[R].
The grepable report has no script results, as in nmap.
.PP
A port whose state is folded into the \f[V]Not shown\f[R] line still
gets its own row when a script gave output for it, in every report, so
the output is not lost; the \f[V]Not shown\f[R] count leaves it out.
nmap drops such output.
.SS Built-in scripts
.PP
The scripts in the \f[V]default\f[R] category are the ones \f[V]-sC\f[R]
and \f[V]-A\f[R] run.
Every one is also in \f[V]safe\f[R].
Each was written for FeroProbe from the protocol\[aq]s specification;
where one of nmap\[aq]s scripts has the same name, FeroProbe\[aq]s does
the same job and lays out its output alike, so reports compare, with the
differences named below.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l l.
