T{
\f[V]banner\f[R]
T}@T{
discovery, safe
T}@T{
open TCP ports
T}@T{
Shows the first line the service sends, without sending anything.
Arguments: \f[V]banner.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 5000),
\f[V]banner.length\f[R] (characters, default 200)
T}
T{
\f[V]dns-nsid\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
53, \f[V]domain\f[R] (TCP or UDP)
T}@T{
Asks a DNS server who it is: the name server identifier (NSID, RFC 5001)
from an EDNS(0) query for \f[V]id.server\f[R], and the CHAOS TXT records
\f[V]id.server\f[R] and \f[V]version.bind\f[R] (RFC 4892), shown as
\f[V]NSID: <id> (<hex>)\f[R], \f[V]id.server:\f[R] and
\f[V]bind.version:\f[R]; only the ones the server gives.
At most three small queries to the scanned port, with the
recursion-desired (RD) flag set as in nmap\[aq]s queries: a server that
rejects EDNS (FORMERR or NOTIMP) is asked for \f[V]id.server\f[R] again
without it.
A reply that carries a question counts only when it is the one asked,
and only the TXT record for that name is read; an error reply without a
question (FORMERR or NOTIMP) only triggers the retry without EDNS.
Over UDP each query is sent twice at most; over TCP with the two-byte
length in front, read in two reads at most within the timeout each.
The NSID and \f[V]id.server\f[R] come from the same reply (unless the
retry without EDNS was needed); \f[V]version.bind\f[R] comes from its
own query (and, over TCP, its own connection), so behind a load balancer
or anycast address it can come from a different server.
The transaction ID is \f[V]0x4650\f[R] (\[dq]FP\[dq]).
Argument: \f[V]dns-nsid.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 5000)
T}
T{
\f[V]ftp-anon\f[R]
T}@T{
default, auth, safe
T}@T{
21, \f[V]ftp\f[R]
T}@T{
Logs in as \f[V]anonymous\f[R] with the password
\f[V]feroprobe\[at]\f[R] and says whether that is allowed, then quits.
Lists no files (nmap\[aq]s does): a listing needs a data connection to a
port the server picks, outside the scan\[aq]s scope.
Argument: \f[V]ftp-anon.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 8000)
T}
T{
\f[V]ftp-syst\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
21, \f[V]ftp\f[R]
T}@T{
Sends SYST and STAT without logging in and shows the answers (many
servers answer STAT only after a login; then only SYST is shown).
Argument: \f[V]ftp-syst.timeout\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]http-headers\f[R]
T}@T{
discovery, safe
T}@T{
web ports
T}@T{
Lists the headers of the front page\[aq]s answer as they came, then the
request used: HEAD, or GET when HEAD gets an error status or no answer.
Follows no redirect.
Not in \f[V]default\f[R], as in nmap.
Arguments: \f[V]http-headers.path\f[R] (default \f[V]/\f[R]),
\f[V]http-headers.useget\f[R] (GET straight away)
T}
T{
\f[V]http-methods\f[R]
T}@T{
default, safe
T}@T{
web ports
T}@T{
Sends one OPTIONS request and lists the methods in the \f[V]Allow\f[R]
header, repeating those beyond GET, HEAD, POST and OPTIONS as
potentially risky.
Tries no method.
Argument: \f[V]http-methods.url-path\f[R] (default \f[V]/\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[V]http-robots.txt\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
web ports
T}@T{
Fetches \f[V]/robots.txt\f[R] and lists its \f[V]Disallow\f[R] paths (at
most 100)
T}
T{
\f[V]http-server-header\f[R]
T}@T{
discovery, safe, version
T}@T{
web ports
T}@T{
One GET of the front page; shows each different \f[V]Server\f[R] header
value (the software the server names), or nothing when there is none.
Follows no redirect.
nmap runs its \f[V]version\f[R] scripts with every \f[V]-sV\f[R] and
lets this one fill in an empty version column; FeroProbe runs it only
when chosen (\f[V]--script http-server-header\f[R] or
\f[V]--script version\f[R]) and leaves the version column to
\f[V]-sV\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]http-title\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
web ports
T}@T{
Fetches a web server\[aq]s front page and shows its title, following
redirects within scope (and saying where the page came from), or where a
redirect it did not follow points.
Argument: \f[V]http-title.path\f[R] (default \f[V]/\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[V]imap-capabilities\f[R]
T}@T{
default, safe
T}@T{
143, 993, \f[V]imap\f[R], \f[V]imaps\f[R]
T}@T{
Sends CAPABILITY and lists the answer; logs in to nothing.
Over TLS on 993 and when \f[V]-sV\f[R] found TLS
T}
T{
\f[V]mysql-info\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
3306, \f[V]mysql\f[R]
T}@T{
Reads the server\[aq]s greeting: protocol, version, thread ID,
capability flags and names, status, salt and authentication plugin, or
the error a server that refuses the connection gives.
Sends nothing
T}
T{
\f[V]nbstat\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
hosts where UDP 137 (or the \f[V]nbstat.port\f[R] port) was scanned and
is open, open|filtered or unfiltered
T}@T{
One NetBIOS NODE STATUS request (as \f[V]nbtstat -A\f[R], RFC 1002),
sent twice at most: the host\[aq]s NetBIOS name, the logged-in user when
the name table has one, and the MAC address the host reports with its
vendor from the OUI list (\f[V]<unknown>\f[R], and no \f[V]mac\f[R] in
XML/JSON, for the all-zero MAC Samba gives); a \f[V]<03>\f[R] name the
machine also holds as a unique \f[V]<00>\f[R] or \f[V]<20>\f[R] name
(its own name or an alias) is not taken for a user; with \f[V]-v\f[R]
the whole name table and its flags.
The transaction ID is \f[V]0x4650\f[R] (\[dq]FP\[dq]).
nmap\[aq]s also runs when only TCP 139 or 445 is open and then sends to
UDP 137 anyway; FeroProbe\[aq]s runs only when its port (UDP 137, or
\f[V]nbstat.port\f[R]) was scanned (\f[V]-sU -p U:137\f[R]), as nothing
goes to ports outside the scan.
nmap\[aq]s shows the name table\[aq]s statistics with \f[V]-vv\f[R];
FeroProbe\[aq]s does not.
Arguments: \f[V]nbstat.port\f[R] (default 137, must be scanned),
\f[V]nbstat.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 5000)
T}
T{
\f[V]ntp-info\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
123/udp, \f[V]ntp\f[R]
T}@T{
One NTP client request (the server\[aq]s receive time) and one
control-message READVAR request (version, system, stratum, reference ID
and the other system variables, which many servers refuse).
Not the \f[V]monlist\f[R] request used for amplification
T}
T{
\f[V]pop3-capabilities\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
110, 995, \f[V]pop3\f[R], \f[V]pop3s\f[R]
T}@T{
Sends CAPA and lists the capabilities, with their arguments in
parentheses, plus APOP when the greeting offers it; logs in to nothing
T}
T{
\f[V]rdp-ntlm-info\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
3389, \f[V]ms-wbt-server\f[R]
T}@T{
Asks for TLS and CredSSP (Network Level Authentication) in the
connection request, sends an NTLM NEGOTIATE inside a CredSSP message
over TLS and shows what the server\[aq]s NTLM CHALLENGE says: target
name, NetBIOS and DNS domain and computer names, DNS tree name, product
version and system time (UTC).
No credentials are sent.
A server that does not offer CredSSP gives no output.
The connection request carries the routing cookie
\f[V]Cookie: mstshash=FeroProbe\f[R].
TLS 1.2 and 1.3 only, as for all FeroProbe TLS, so old servers with TLS
1.0 only give no output.
Argument: \f[V]rdp-ntlm-info.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 8000)
T}
T{
\f[V]rpcinfo\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
111/tcp and udp, \f[V]rpcbind\f[R]
T}@T{
Asks the portmapper for its registered RPC programs (DUMP, as
\f[V]rpcinfo -p\f[R]) and lists program, versions, port and protocol,
and service name.
The ports it lists are not contacted
T}
T{
\f[V]smb2-security-mode\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
hosts with 445 or 139 open
T}@T{
One SMB2 NEGOTIATE (see \f[V]fero.smb.negotiate\f[R]) and whether the
server signs messages: enabled and required, enabled but not required,
or disabled, under the dialect it chose (\f[V]3:1:1\f[R]).
Arguments: \f[V]smbport\f[R], \f[V]smb2-security-mode.timeout\f[R] (ms,
default 8000)
T}
T{
\f[V]smb2-time\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
hosts with 445 or 139 open
T}@T{
The server\[aq]s clock and start time from its NEGOTIATE answer
(\f[V]N/A\f[R] when it gives none, as current Windows does), in UTC.
nmap\[aq]s also adds a \f[V]clock-skew\f[R] line to the host\[aq]s
script results; FeroProbe\[aq]s does not yet.
Arguments: \f[V]smbport\f[R], \f[V]smb2-time.timeout\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]smtp-commands\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
25, 465, 587, \f[V]smtp\f[R], \f[V]submission\f[R]
T}@T{
Sends \f[V]EHLO feroprobe.invalid\f[R] and lists the server\[aq]s name
and extensions; QUIT.
Over TLS on 465.
\f[V]smtp.domain\f[R] (nmap\[aq]s name) sends another name instead.
nmap\[aq]s also sends HELP, and by default the target\[aq]s own name;
FeroProbe\[aq]s sends no HELP
T}
T{
\f[V]ssh-hostkey\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
22, \f[V]ssh\f[R]
T}@T{
Shows each host key\[aq]s size, MD5 fingerprint and type, as nmap\[aq]s
does, and with \f[V]-vv\f[R] the key.
\f[V]ssh-hostkey.format\f[R] (or nmap\[aq]s \f[V]ssh_hostkey\f[R]) picks
\f[V]md5\f[R], \f[V]sha256\f[R] (as \f[V]ssh-keygen -l\f[R] prints it),
\f[V]full\f[R] or \f[V]all\f[R]; nmap\[aq]s \f[V]visual\f[R] and
\f[V]bubble\f[R] forms are not offered.
One connection per key type the server offers; each stops at the
server\[aq]s key exchange reply, before any login.
The client announces itself as
\f[V]SSH-2.0-FeroProbe_Scripting_Engine\f[R].
Key exchange: curve25519 or the Diffie-Hellman groups 14, 16, 18 and 1.
Argument: \f[V]ssh-hostkey.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 10000)
T}
T{
\f[V]ssl-cert\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
TLS ports
T}@T{
Shows the certificate of a TLS port: subject, alternative names, issuer,
key type and size, signature algorithm, validity dates, SHA-1 and
SHA-256 fingerprints, and with \f[V]-v\f[R] the PEM.
Direct TLS only for now (no STARTTLS); nmap\[aq]s also shows MD5,
FeroProbe\[aq]s SHA-256 instead
T}
T{
\f[V]ssl-date\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
TLS ports
T}@T{
Sends a TLS 1.2 ClientHello and reads the time the server puts in the
first four bytes of its ServerHello random, as
\f[V]2026-11-18T10:00:00+00:00; +5s from scanner time.\f[R] No handshake
is finished.
A second hello checks that the value moves with the clock (and when both
answers came within one second, a third one two seconds later, so that a
field that never changes is not taken for a clock); most current servers
fill it with random bytes, and then the script says
\f[V]TLS randomness does not represent time\f[R].
A ServerHello split across several records is read; only its first 10
bytes are read, within one timeout.
The hello\[aq]s random carries \f[V]FeroProbeSSLDate\f[R] after the time
field.
Direct TLS only (no STARTTLS, which nmap\[aq]s also tries); nmap\[aq]s
also adds a \f[V]clock-skew\f[R] line to the host\[aq]s script results,
FeroProbe\[aq]s does not yet.
Argument: \f[V]ssl-date.timeout\f[R] (ms, default 8000)
T}
T{
\f[V]sshv1\f[R]
T}@T{
default, safe
T}@T{
22, \f[V]ssh\f[R]
T}@T{
Says whether the server still accepts the obsolete SSH protocol 1, from
the version in its greeting; sends nothing.
nmap\[aq]s also reads the version 1 keys and ciphers
T}
T{
\f[V]vnc-info\f[R]
T}@T{
default, discovery, safe
T}@T{
5900-5910, \f[V]vnc\f[R]
T}@T{
Answers the RFB version handshake and lists the security types offered,
with a warning when \[dq]None\[dq] (no authentication) is one; chooses
none, so nothing logs in
T}
T{
\f[V]x11-access\f[R]
T}@T{
default, auth, safe
T}@T{
6000-6009, \f[V]X11\f[R]
T}@T{
Sends an X11 connection setup without credentials and says whether the
server lets anyone connect; closes at once
T}
.TE
.PP
\[dq]Web ports\[dq] are the usual HTTP and HTTPS ports (80, 443, 8080,
8443 and others) and ports whose service is \f[V]http\f[R],
\f[V]https\f[R] or similar; \[dq]TLS ports\[dq] are ports where
\f[V]-sV\f[R] found TLS, or whose number or service is a TLS one.
A script whose rule needs a service name sees the one \f[V]-sV\f[R]
found, or without \f[V]-sV\f[R] the name of the port number, as in nmap.
.PP
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]default\f[R] category has more scripts (other SMB ones,
DNS and others); FeroProbe\[aq]s grows release by release.
Scripts of nmap\[aq]s that would reach beyond the scanned host, such as
\f[V]http-open-proxy\f[R] or \f[V]socks-open-proxy\f[R], which ask the
target to fetch an outside site, are not planned for the default set.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ feroprobe -sV -p 80,443 --script http-title,ssl-cert 192.0.2.10
PORT       STATE          SERVICE        VERSION
80/tcp     open           http           nginx 1.24.0
|_http-title: Did not follow redirect to https://www.example.test/
443/tcp    open           ssl/http       nginx 1.24.0
| http-title: Example
|_Requested resource was https://192.0.2.10/start
| ssl-cert: Subject: commonName=www.example.test/organizationName=Example
| Subject Alternative Name: DNS:www.example.test, DNS:example.test
| Issuer: countryName=US/organizationName=Example CA/commonName=Example CA R1
| Public Key type: ec
| Public Key bits: 256
| Signature Algorithm: ecdsa-with-SHA384
| Not valid before: 2026-09-01T00:00:00
| Not valid after:  2026-11-30T23:59:59
| SHA-1:   3c1f...
|_SHA-256: 9a07...
\f[R]
.fi
.SH TIMING AND PERFORMANCE
.SS Timing templates (\f[V]-T<0-5>\f[R])
.PP
\f[V]-T0\f[R] to \f[V]-T5\f[R], or \f[V]-T\f[R] with a name
(\f[V]-T paranoid\f[R] ...
\f[V]-T insane\f[R]), pick a bundle of timing settings, as in nmap.
\f[V]-T3\f[R] (normal) is the default.
Any of \f[V]--timeout\f[R], the RTT timeout options,
\f[V]--retries\f[R], \f[V]-c\f[R]/\f[V]--concurrency\f[R],
\f[V]--min-parallelism\f[R], \f[V]--max-rate\f[R],
\f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] and \f[V]--max-scan-delay\f[R] given explicitly
overrides the template\[aq]s value for that setting, so
\f[V]-T4 --max-rate 2000\f[R] is aggressive with a lower packet rate.
Given more than once, the last \f[V]-T\f[R] wins, as in nmap.
.PP
.TS
