.TE
.PP
The RTT timeouts, retries and maximum scan delays are nmap\[aq]s for
each template.
As in nmap, no template limits the packet rate: only an explicit
\f[V]--max-rate\f[R] does.
Most retries is a cap: a probe is resent once unless its host shows
losses (see Resends and delays).
The scan delay also sets a floor under every measured timeout, so
\f[V]-T0\f[R] and \f[V]-T1\f[R] probes wait at least their delay.
.SS How long a probe waits
.PP
Like nmap, FeroProbe adapts its timeouts to the network.
Every answer (a SYN-ACK, a reset, an ICMP error, a UDP reply, an echo
reply, an ARP or neighbor discovery reply) gives a round-trip time, and
each host keeps a smoothed round-trip time and its variance, the way TCP
does (RFC 6298).
A probe to that host waits the smoothed time plus four times the
variance, kept between \f[V]--min-rtt-timeout\f[R] and
\f[V]--max-rtt-timeout\f[R] and never below the scan delay.
A host that has not answered yet waits as long as the scan\[aq]s hosts
do together, and before any answer at all a probe waits
\f[V]--initial-rtt-timeout\f[R].
The estimates carry over from host discovery to the port scan, OS
detection, version detection\[aq]s connections and traceroute.
Round trips far outside the estimate (over 8 s, or a sudden jump of more
than 1.5 s) are ignored, as in nmap.
.PP
So on a fast local network a closed or filtered port costs about 100 ms
instead of a second, and on a slow link the timeout grows to fit.
\f[V]--timeout\f[R] turns this off: it fixes all three limits to one
value.
.PP
ARP and neighbour discovery answers come from the link, not the host, so
they do not feed the estimates; their sweep waits as long as a host that
has not answered yet.
Reverse DNS through \f[V]--dns-servers\f[R] waits the initial timeout,
at most 5 s, since the name servers are not scan targets; the system
resolver keeps its own timeouts.
.SS Congestion control
.PP
Also like nmap, the scans hold back how many probes may wait for an
answer at once, with congestion windows like TCP\[aq]s: one per host and
one for all hosts together.
Each starts at 10 probes.
Every answer widens it (by one probe per answer up to 75, then about one
probe per window\[aq]s worth of answers; faster when many probes go
unanswered, and with \f[V]-T4\f[R] and \f[V]-T5\f[R]).
An answer to a resent probe means the first one was lost: the host\[aq]s
window then drops to one probe (or \f[V]--min-parallelism\f[R]) and the
group\[aq]s halves, but no lower.
A probe that only times out moves no window.
ICMP errors to resent probes are left out in the scans where silence
means \f[V]filtered\f[R], since routers rate-limit them.
The group window only applies while two or more hosts are still being
scanned.
.PP
\f[V]-c\f[R]/\f[V]--concurrency\f[R] caps both windows, and
\f[V]--min-parallelism\f[R] is their floor.
The windows only ever hold probes back: \f[V]--max-rate\f[R] and
\f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] still apply on top, so gentle timing stays
gentle.
Only \f[V]--min-rate\f[R] lets a probe through a full window, to keep up
its rate, as in nmap.
.PP
This applies to the raw TCP, SCTP, IP protocol, connect and UDP scans,
including the TCP and UDP probes of host discovery.
Because only answers widen the windows, a host that answers nothing is
scanned slowly: about 10 probes per timeout.
A thousand filtered ports with one retry take a few minutes at the
defaults, as with nmap; \f[V]-T4\f[R], \f[V]--min-rate\f[R], or
\f[V]-Pn\f[R] avoided on dead hosts speed this up.
.SS Resends and delays
.PP
As in nmap, how often a probe without an answer is sent again depends on
its host.
At first a probe is resent once.
When a host answers a resent probe, the first copy or its answer was
lost, so that host\[aq]s probes may be resent one time more than the
highest resend it answered, up to \f[V]--max-retries\f[R] (10 by
default).
Probes still waiting for an answer at that point are sent again too.
A host that loses nothing costs one resend per silent port, and a host
that drops packets, or rate-limits its resets or ICMP errors, is probed
until it answers.
.PP
Resends go before new probes.
The first time a host has a probe due for a higher try than any it was
sent before (its first resend, its first second resend, and so on), the
scan sends that host nothing for one second, as nmap does to detect rate
limiting: a host that limits how fast it answers gets to recover, and
answers that were only slow come in before anything is resent.
A scan of a single probe skips the pause.
.PP
A lossy host also gets a delay between its probes.
When more than 10 of its answers since the delay last changed came only
after a resend, and they are more than 30 % of its answers (40 % with
\f[V]-T4\f[R] and \f[V]-T5\f[R]), or when it answers only after more
than 3 resends (4 with \f[V]-T4\f[R] and \f[V]-T5\f[R]), the delay goes
up: to 5 ms at first (50 ms for UDP), then doubling, up to 1 s and no
more than \f[V]--max-scan-delay\f[R].
It never drops below \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R].
Routers and firewalls that rate-limit their resets (closed TCP ports) or
ICMP errors (closed UDP ports) then get their answers counted instead of
being reported \f[V]filtered\f[R] or \f[V]open|filtered\f[R], at the
price of a slower scan.
.PP
This applies to the raw TCP, SCTP, IP protocol, connect and UDP scans
and their host discovery probes.
ARP, ICMP echo, OS detection, traceroute and reverse DNS resend once
(none with \f[V]--max-retries 0\f[R]).
.SS Timing options
.SS \f[V]--initial-rtt-timeout <time>\f[R]
.PP
How long a probe waits before any round trip has been measured
(\f[V]500ms\f[R], \f[V]2s\f[R]; a bare number is seconds).
Default from the template: 1 s.
Must be positive.
If it is above the maximum, the maximum rises to it; below the minimum,
the minimum falls to it, as in nmap.
.SS \f[V]--min-rtt-timeout <time>\f[R]
.PP
The shortest a probe\[aq]s adaptive timeout may get.
Default from the template: 100 ms (50 ms with \f[V]-T5\f[R]).
The maximum and the initial timeout rise to it where they are lower.
Raise it on a network that drops answers under load, where short
timeouts lose them.
.SS \f[V]--max-rtt-timeout <time>\f[R]
.PP
The longest a probe\[aq]s adaptive timeout may get.
Default from the template: 10 s.
The minimum and the initial timeout fall to it where they are higher.
It must be at least 5 ms, and below 20 ms it warns that accuracy may
suffer, as in nmap.
.PP
When more than one of the three is given, they are applied in nmap\[aq]s
order: initial, then minimum, then maximum.
.SS \f[V]--timeout <ms>\f[R]
.PP
A fixed timeout for every probe, in milliseconds: it sets the minimum,
maximum and initial RTT timeouts all to this value, so measured round
trips no longer change it.
FeroProbe\[aq]s own option, kept from before the timeouts adapted; the
RTT options given with it still apply on top.
Below 20 ms it warns that accuracy may suffer.
.SS \f[V]--retries <tries>\f[R], \f[V]--max-retries <tries>\f[R]
.PP
The most times a probe that got no answer is sent again.
Default from the template: 10 (6 with \f[V]-T4\f[R], 2 with
\f[V]-T5\f[R]).
\f[V]--max-retries\f[R] is nmap\[aq]s name for it and works the same.
As in nmap it is a cap: a probe is resent once, and more often only to
hosts that lose probes (see Resends and delays).
\f[V]0\f[R] turns resends off.
.SS \f[V]-c <number>\f[R], \f[V]--concurrency <number>\f[R]
.PP
The most probes waiting for an answer at once: the cap on the congestion
windows (see Congestion control), and for connect and UDP scans also the
number of sockets open at once.
Default from the template: 500.
nmap\[aq]s names \f[V]--max-parallelism <number>\f[R] and
\f[V]-M <number>\f[R] work too.
It must be at least 1, warns above 900, and warns when the number is
above the system\[aq]s open-file limit (\f[V]ulimit -n\f[R]).
.SS \f[V]--min-parallelism <number>\f[R]
.PP
Keep at least this many probes waiting at once: the congestion windows
never shrink below it, as in nmap, and it raises the template\[aq]s
\f[V]-c\f[R] (or the one \f[V]--min-rate\f[R] picked) to this number if
that is lower.
It must be at least 1, warns above 100, and is an error above an
explicit \f[V]-c\f[R]/\f[V]--max-parallelism\f[R], with nmap\[aq]s
message.
.SS \f[V]--max-rate <number>\f[R]
.PP
Send at most this many raw-socket packets per second (SYN and other raw
TCP probes, SCTP, IP protocol, ICMP, ARP and NDP).
By default there is no limit, as in nmap: the congestion windows and the
scan delay pace the probes.
One rate holds for the whole run, across all scans and hosts.
Connect and UDP probes are bounded by the congestion windows,
\f[V]--concurrency\f[R] and \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] instead.
.SS \f[V]--min-rate <number>\f[R]
.PP
Send at least this many probes per second.
It raises \f[V]--max-rate\f[R] to at least this number, shortens a
template\[aq]s scan delay to fit, lets probes through a full congestion
window whenever the scan falls behind the rate, and lets enough connect
and UDP probes be in flight to keep up the rate even when every probe
waits out its initial timeout (up to the open-file limit, with a warning
when that limit, or an explicit \f[V]--concurrency\f[R], may hold the
rate down).
It is an error when it is 0, above an explicit \f[V]--max-rate\f[R], or
when an explicit \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] is too long for it.
.SS \f[V]--scan-delay <time>\f[R]
.PP
Wait at least this long between probes, across all scan techniques
(\f[V]500ms\f[R], \f[V]2s\f[R], \f[V]5m\f[R]; a bare number is seconds).
Default from the template: none for \f[V]-T3\f[R] and up.
The scan may raise the delay for a host that loses probes (see Resends
and delays), but never lowers it.
.SS \f[V]--max-scan-delay <time>\f[R]
.PP
The longest delay between probes the scan may set on its own for a host
that loses probes (\f[V]100ms\f[R], \f[V]1s\f[R]; a bare number is
seconds).
Default from the template: 1 s, or for TCP, SCTP and IP protocol probes
10 ms with \f[V]-T4\f[R] and 5 ms with \f[V]-T5\f[R].
An explicit \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] above it raises it, as in nmap;
\f[V]--max-scan-delay\f[R] given explicitly applies to every scan type.
It never takes the delay below \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--defeat-rst-ratelimit\f[R]
.PP
In a SYN scan, a reset that answers a resent probe no longer slows the
scan down.
Many hosts rate-limit the resets they send for closed ports, and the
scan otherwise reads the missing ones as losses and backs off.
The price, as in nmap: a closed port whose reset was held back may be
reported \f[V]filtered\f[R], and the port table then says
\f[V]Some closed ports may be reported as filtered due to --defeat-rst-ratelimit\f[R].
It is refused with any other TCP scan type unless \f[V]--open\f[R] is
given.
\f[V]--open\f[R] turns it on by itself, as in nmap, since closed and
filtered ports are not shown then.
.SS \f[V]--host-timeout <time>\f[R]
.PP
Give up on a host whose port scans and detection take longer than this
(\f[V]30s\f[R], \f[V]5m\f[R]).
Hosts then run on their own clocks, up to 32 at once, sharing
\f[V]--concurrency\f[R] and the packet rate.
A timed-out host is reported without port or OS results:
\f[V]Skipping host <addr> due to host timeout\f[R] in normal output,
\f[V]Status: Timeout\f[R] in grepable output and
\f[V]timedout=\[dq]true\[dq]\f[R] in XML.
It is not traced.
\f[V]0\f[R] means no limit, as in nmap.
.PP
The host timeout does not yet cover scripts (\f[V]--script\f[R]): a host
that finished its scans in time runs its scripts with their own limit,
\f[V]--script-timeout\f[R] (ten minutes by default), per script.
Use that option to bound the script phase.
.SS \f[V]--min-hostgroup <size>\f[R], \f[V]--max-hostgroup <size>\f[R]
.PP
As in nmap, hosts are pinged in batches of up to 4096 targets, then
port-scanned, traced and reported a group at a time; each group\[aq]s
results are printed and written to the report files as soon as it
finishes.
The group size is nmap\[aq]s: 128 hosts for UDP or SCTP scans, 1024
divided by the TCP port count (at least 64) for TCP scans, and 16
otherwise.
A TCP scan of more than 1000 ports at \f[V]-T4\f[R] or slower starts
with a quick group of 4 hosts (8 at \f[V]-T4\f[R]) to show results
early.
These two options hold the size between \f[V]--min-hostgroup\f[R]
(default 1) and \f[V]--max-hostgroup\f[R] (default 100000).
A larger group can be faster; a smaller one reports sooner.
\f[V]--min-hostgroup\f[R] above \f[V]--max-hostgroup\f[R] is an error,
and above 100 it warns, as in nmap.
A minimum above 4096 also raises the discovery batch to it.
.SH FIREWALL/IDS EVASION AND SPOOFING
.SS Interface and source port
.PP
These two work against any target, like in nmap; they do not need
\f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-e <interface>\f[R]
.PP
Send and receive on this network interface (\f[V]eth0\f[R],
\f[V]wlan0\f[R], \f[V]tun0\f[R], as \f[V]ip link\f[R] names them), as
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-e\f[R] does.
Every socket of the scan is bound to it: raw probes, the packet capture
of IPv6 OS detection, ARP and neighbour discovery, connect scans, UDP
probes, version detection and the reverse DNS queries of
\f[V]--dns-servers\f[R].
The interface\[aq]s own address is the source of the probes (unless
\f[V]-S\f[R] gives another), and only its networks count as local for
ARP and neighbour discovery: a target on another interface\[aq]s network
is reached through \f[V]-e\f[R]\[aq]s interface, as if it were on that
link.
Forward lookups of target names use the system resolver, which is not
bound.
.PP
It is an error when there is no such interface.
Linux and macOS only.
Binding a socket to an interface needs root (or \f[V]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R])
on Linux kernels older than 5.7; newer kernels allow it to any user.
With \f[V]-v\f[R], FeroProbe prints the interface it uses.
.SS \f[V]-g <portnum>\f[R], \f[V]--source-port <portnum>\f[R]
.PP
Send probes from this source port, as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-g\f[R] does: some
firewalls let in anything from port 53 (DNS) or 20 (FTP data).
It applies to:
.IP \[bu] 2
the raw TCP scans (\f[V]-sS\f[R], \f[V]-sA\f[R], \f[V]-sF\f[R], ...,
\f[V]--scanflags\f[R]), SCTP scans (\f[V]-sY\f[R], \f[V]-sZ\f[R]) and
the TCP, UDP and SCTP probes of the IP protocol scan (\f[V]-sO\f[R]);
.IP \[bu] 2
the raw TCP pings (\f[V]-PS\f[R], \f[V]-PA\f[R] as root) and UDP pings
(\f[V]-PU\f[R]);
.IP \[bu] 2
the UDP scan (\f[V]-sU\f[R]);
.IP \[bu] 2
OS detection (\f[V]-O\f[R]).
.PP
As in nmap, it does not apply to the connect scan (\f[V]-sT\f[R];
FeroProbe warns, with nmap\[aq]s message, and keeps the system\[aq]s
ports), to unprivileged TCP pings (connects), to version detection, to
traceroute (whose source port tells its probes apart) or to DNS.
\f[V]-g 0\f[R] warns that port 0 may not work; the UDP probes, which go
through the system\[aq]s sockets, cannot be sent from port 0 and keep a
random port (the warning says so).
.PP
FeroProbe already sends every raw probe of a scan from one source port
(random, from 40000-59999), so \f[V]-g\f[R] only fixes which one; the
probe signature (\f[V]docs/responsible-use.md\f[R]) is unchanged.
UDP probes from a fixed port share it between sockets
(\f[V]SO_REUSEADDR\f[R]), each connected to a different target port.
If another program holds the port, the UDP scan fails with an error
naming it.
On Windows, sockets cannot share a UDP port that way, so \f[V]-g\f[R]
does not apply to UDP probes there (FeroProbe warns).
.SS Fragmentation, decoys and spoofed source
.PP
FeroProbe has nmap\[aq]s fragmentation, decoy and spoofed-source options
for testing your own firewalls and intrusion detection, and for
authorized penetration tests.
Unlike nmap, they are gated by an authorization model.
.SS Authorization model
.IP \[bu] 2
The options work only with a raw TCP scan (\f[V]-sS\f[R], \f[V]-sF\f[R],
\f[V]-sN\f[R], \f[V]-sX\f[R], \f[V]-sA\f[R], \f[V]-sW\f[R],
\f[V]-sM\f[R], or \f[V]--scanflags\f[R]), on IPv4 targets.
Anything else is refused with an error, with or without the flag below.
.IP \[bu] 2
Every target, every decoy and the spoofed source must be in a private
range: RFC 1918 (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), carrier-grade NAT
(100.64/10, RFC 6598), loopback (127/8) or link-local (169.254/16).
(FeroProbe also treats IPv6 loopback, unique-local \f[V]fc00::/7\f[R]
and link-local \f[V]fe80::/10\f[R] as private, though the options are
IPv4 only.)
.IP \[bu] 2
A public address anywhere among them is refused, with a message naming
it, unless you pass \f[B]\f[VB]--allow-public-evasion\f[B]\f[R].
That flag is the deliberate opt-in that says you have permission to test
those networks.
Private ranges never need it.
.IP \[bu] 2
The address check runs before the privilege check, so a public address
is refused whether or not raw sockets are available.
A real run then needs raw sockets; \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] needs none and
applies the same address check.
.PP
The reasons are in docs/responsible-use.md.
.SS \f[V]-f\f[R] (fragment packets)
.PP
Split each raw IPv4 probe into IP fragments that carry 8 bytes of
payload each, so a TCP header spans three fragments.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]--mtu\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--mtu <n>\f[R]
.PP
Fragment raw IPv4 probes with \f[V]n\f[R] bytes of payload per fragment.
\f[V]n\f[R] must be a positive multiple of 8.
.SS \f[V]-D <decoy1[,decoy2][,ME],...>\f[R], \f[V]--decoys\f[R]
.PP
Send each probe from every decoy source address as well, so the target
sees the scan coming from several hosts.
Decoys are IPv4 addresses.
nmap uses the token \f[V]ME\f[R] to place the real scanner in the list;
FeroProbe accepts \f[V]ME\f[R] anywhere but always sends the decoy
copies first and the real probe last.
Answers to the decoys go to the decoy addresses.
.SS \f[V]-S <IP_Address>\f[R], \f[V]--source\f[R]
.PP
Send the raw IPv4 probes from this forged source address.
Answers go to that address, not to FeroProbe, so the scan is blind
unless FeroProbe sits on the return path; it is mainly for watching what
your IDS makes of the traffic, as in nmap.
.SS \f[V]--spoof-mac <MAC address, prefix, or vendor name>\f[R]
.PP
Send IPv4 probes and ARP requests in Ethernet frames from this hardware
address instead of the card\[aq]s own, as nmap does.
The argument is read as nmap reads it:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]0\f[R]: a random address (FeroProbe clears the multicast bit, which
a source address must not have),
.IP \[bu] 2
hex digits, optionally separated by \f[V]:\f[R] (also \f[V]-\f[R] or
\f[V].\f[R]): a whole address, or its first bytes with the rest random
(\f[V]00:0C:29\f[R] looks like VMware),
.IP \[bu] 2
anything else: a vendor name, matched without regard to case against the
built-in vendor table, then against the installed IEEE list (see
\f[V]--oui-file\f[R]), whose prefix is used with three random bytes
(\f[V]--spoof-mac Raspberry\f[R]).
.PP
FeroProbe prints the address it chose
(\f[V]Spoofing MAC address 00:0C:29:5A:11:03 (VMware)\f[R]).
The next hop\[aq]s address comes from the kernel\[aq]s neighbour table,
or else from an ARP request sent from the spoofed address.
Answers to the spoofed address are picked up off the card (which is told
to accept that address, or put in promiscuous mode) and handed to
FeroProbe through the loopback interface, with any checksum the card
left unfinished filled in.
TCP answers go with a deliberately wrong checksum: FeroProbe\[aq]s raw
sockets read them first, and this host\[aq]s TCP then drops them instead
of resetting each SYN-ACK from the real MAC address (nmap, reading
answers with pcap, draws no reset either).
Packets of a connection the system itself holds (version
detection\[aq]s, once the target has learnt the spoofed address from the
ARP requests) are passed on intact.
With \f[V]-e\f[R], the probes leave through that interface, and the
sockets that read IPv4 answers are not bound to it, since the answers
come in through loopback.
A strict reverse-path filter (\f[V]net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=1\f[R])
drops those; FeroProbe warns, and only answers sent to the card\[aq]s
own address are then seen.
.PP
Linux only, needs raw sockets.
It covers raw IPv4 probes of every kind (the port scans, host discovery,
OS detection, traceroute), UDP scan probes (which then go out on a raw
socket) and ARP discovery.
It does not cover IPv6 probes, the TCP connect scan, version detection
or DNS, whose packets the system sends; FeroProbe warns when the scan
includes any of them.
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] adds \f[V]ether-src=<MAC>\f[R] to each probe
sent in a spoofed frame.
Like every SENT line it is printed just before the send, so it shows a
frame FeroProbe attempted to send, not proof that the frame left.
Like the other evasion options, public targets need
\f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R]
.PP
Authorize the options above against public targets, decoys and spoofed
sources you have permission to test.
.PP
With \f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] (and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R]), each fragment
gets its own line, so you see exactly what goes on the wire: the first
fragment as \f[V]TCP src:sport > dst:dport (frag)\f[R], the later ones
as \f[V]IP src > dst proto 6 frag offset=N\f[R], with a trailing
\f[V]+\f[R] while more fragments follow.
nmap shows only the unfragmented probe there; see Differences from nmap.
.SS Packet contents
.PP
These options change what goes into the probes.
Unlike the options above they need no authorization; \f[V]--badsum\f[R]
needs raw sockets.
.PP
They apply, as in nmap, to the raw TCP scans, the UDP scan, the SCTP
scans, the IP protocol scan, the discovery pings and (all but
\f[V]--ip-options\f[R]) traceroute.
OS detection keeps its own packets: it takes \f[V]--ttl\f[R] but no
payload, options or bad checksum.
The connect scan and version detection use the system\[aq]s TCP stack,
so none of them apply; FeroProbe warns when the TCP scan is
\f[V]-sT\f[R].
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] show what was added:
\f[V]len=N\f[R] for TCP data, \f[V]ttl=N\f[R], \f[V]ipopts={...}\f[R],
\f[V]badsum\f[R], and \f[V]via <hop>\f[R] for a source route.
The options read as in nmap, such as
\f[V]ipopts={ NOP RR{#0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0}}\f[R], with \f[V]#\f[R] before
the slot the pointer names; received packets show their options too, so
a record route comes back filled in.
Like nmap, \f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] and \f[V]-d\f[R] also print the
options once before the scan, as bytes and as text.
.SS \f[V]--data <hex string>\f[R]
.PP
Add these bytes after the header of each probe, written
\f[V]0xdeadbeef\f[R], \f[V]\[rs]xde\[rs]xad\[rs]xbe\[rs]xef\f[R] or
\f[V]deadbeef\f[R].
They replace the payload of ICMP echo requests and of UDP probes (nmap
does the same with its UDP payloads), so those probes no longer carry
FeroProbe\[aq]s \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] and \f[V]FeroProbeScan/1\f[R]
markers; the TCP window mark stays (see docs/responsible-use.md).
Payloads over 1400 bytes get nmap\[aq]s warning that they may not be
sent successfully.
Only one of \f[V]--data\f[R], \f[V]--data-string\f[R] and
\f[V]--data-length\f[R] may be given.
.SS \f[V]--data-string <string>\f[R]
.PP
Like \f[V]--data\f[R], with the bytes of this text.
.SS \f[V]--data-length <number>\f[R]
.PP
Like \f[V]--data\f[R], with this many random bytes (0 to 65435), drawn
once for the whole scan as nmap does.
.SS \f[V]--ip-options <options>\f[R]
.PP
Send IPv4 probes with these IP header options, in nmap\[aq]s notation:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]R\f[R]: record route (room for 9 addresses),
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]T\f[R]: internet timestamp; \f[V]U\f[R]: timestamps with addresses,
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]L <hop> [<hop> ...]\f[R]: loose source route;
\f[V]S <hop> ...\f[R]: strict source route.
Up to 8 hops; the packet goes to the first hop and the target takes the
last slot,
.IP \[bu] 2
raw bytes: \f[V]\[rs]x01\[rs]x07\[rs]x04...\f[R] in hex,
\f[V]\[rs]1\[rs]7\f[R] in decimal, and \f[V]*N\f[R] to repeat the last
byte (\f[V]\[rs]x01*4\f[R] is four NOPs).
.PP
The result is padded to a multiple of 4 bytes and may be at most 40.
IPv4 only: probes to IPv6 targets go without them, with a warning.
With \f[V]-f\f[R]/\f[V]--mtu\f[R] every fragment carries the whole
header, options included, as in nmap.
Many routers drop packets with source routes.
On systems other than Linux only the probes FeroProbe builds whole
(\f[V]-sO\f[R], \f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--mtu\f[R], \f[V]-D\f[R],
\f[V]-S\f[R]) carry the options, with a warning.
.SS \f[V]--ttl <value>\f[R]
.PP
Send probes with this IP time-to-live (IPv6 hop limit), 0 to 255,
instead of a random one from 37 to 59.
The system refuses an IPv4 TTL of 0 on sockets whose header it writes,
so with \f[V]--ttl 0\f[R] those probes go with TTL 1 (FeroProbe warns);
probes it builds whole and IPv6 probes carry 0.
.SS \f[V]--badsum\f[R]
.PP
Send TCP, UDP, SCTP, ICMP and IGMP probes with a checksum one less than
the right one, as nmap does.
Hosts drop such packets, so an answer usually comes from a firewall or
IDS that did not check the checksum.
UDP scan probes then go out on a raw socket from the probe\[aq]s port.
It needs raw sockets: without them FeroProbe warns and sends correct
checksums.
ICMPv6 checksums are always written by the kernel, so IPv6 echo requests
keep theirs.
.SH OUTPUT
.SS Normal output
.PP
The default report on standard output is nmap\[aq]s \[dq]normal\[dq]
layout:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Starting FeroProbe 0.5.0

Scan report for example.org (203.0.113.5)
Host is up, syn-ack (0.85 ms latency).
Not shown: 96 closed ports
PORT       STATE          SERVICE        REASON
22/tcp     open           ssh            syn-ack
80/tcp     open           http           syn-ack
443/tcp    open           https          syn-ack
8080/tcp   filtered       http-proxy     no-response

FeroProbe done: 1 host(s), 1 up, 100 port(s) each, in 1.62s
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each host that is up gets a \[dq]Scan report\[dq] with the reason it is
up and its latency, its MAC address and vendor when ARP or neighbour
discovery found it, the port table, and the OS guess and route when
asked for.
Hosts that are down are not listed (with \f[V]-Pn\f[R] every target is).
When a single target seems down, a note suggests \f[V]-Pn\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Not shown rule.\f[R] As in nmap, a non-open state is folded into a
single \f[V]Not shown: 96 closed ports\f[R] line (states with the most
ports first) only when a host has more than 25 ports in it; smaller
groups are listed port by port.
So \f[V]-p 80\f[R] on a closed port shows \f[V]80/tcp closed\f[R].
Each \f[V]-v\f[R] raises the limit by another 25 (50 with \f[V]-v\f[R],
75 with \f[V]-vv\f[R]), and from \f[V]-vvv\f[R] on,
\f[V]open|filtered\f[R] ports are never folded, since they may be open.
When every port is folded, the line reads
\f[V]All 1000 scanned ports are closed\f[R] (or
\f[V]... are in ignored states.\f[R] followed by the \f[V]Not shown\f[R]
line).
Open ports are never folded.
.SS Output options
.SS \f[V]-o <format>\f[R], \f[V]--output <format>\f[R]
.PP
The format of the report on standard output: \f[V]table\f[R] (normal
output, the default), \f[V]xml\f[R], \f[V]grepable\f[R] or
\f[V]json\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-oN <file>\f[R]
.PP
Also write the normal report to a file.
As in nmap, the file starts with a comment line naming the version, the
start time and the command line
(\f[V]# FeroProbe 0.5.0 scan initiated ... as: feroprobe -oN scan.nmap ...\f[R]),
which \f[V]--resume\f[R] reads, and ends with
\f[V]# FeroProbe done at ...\f[R] instead of the screen\[aq]s summary
line.
.SS \f[V]-oX <file>\f[R]
.PP
Also write the XML report to a file, in nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-oX\f[R] layout
(including \f[V]<trace>\f[R] for traceroute), so tools that import nmap
XML can read it.
The root element says \f[V]scanner=\[dq]feroprobe\[dq]\f[R]; see
\f[V]--nmap-label\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-oS <file>\f[R]
.PP
Also write the normal report to a file in \[dq]script kiddie\[dq]
spelling, as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-oS\f[R] does: at random, letters become
look-alikes (\f[V]4\f[R] for \f[V]A\f[R], \f[V]3\f[R] for \f[V]e\f[R],
\f[V]0\f[R] for \f[V]o\f[R], \f[V]$\f[R] or \f[V]z\f[R] for \f[V]s\f[R],
\f[V]!\f[R], \f[V]|\f[R] or \f[V]1\f[R] for \f[V]i\f[R], and \f[V]s\f[R]
for \f[V]z\f[R]) or change case.
Fingerprint lines (the \f[V]OS:\f[R] lines and the unrecognized service
replies) are left as they are, so they can still be copied.
It exists for parity with nmap; \f[V]-oA\f[R] does not include it.
.SS \f[V]-oG <file>\f[R]
.PP
Also write the grepable report to a file: one line per host, like
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-oG\f[R], for \f[V]grep\f[R] and \f[V]awk\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-oJ <file>\f[R]
.PP
Also write the JSON report to a file.
JSON is FeroProbe\[aq]s own format: a top-level object with
\f[V]scanner\f[R], \f[V]version\f[R], \f[V]scan_types\f[R],
\f[V]elapsed_secs\f[R] and a \f[V]hosts\f[R] array, each host with its
address, names, up reason, ports (number, protocol, state, reason,
round-trip time, service and version), MAC address, OS guess and trace
where present.
.SS \f[V]-oA <basename>\f[R]
.PP
Write \f[V]<basename>.nmap\f[R], \f[V]<basename>.xml\f[R] and
\f[V]<basename>.gnmap\f[R] (normal, XML and grepable) at once.
.PP
A file name of \f[V]-\f[R] sends that report to standard output in place
of the usual report there; only one report can go to standard output.
Two reports cannot share a file (FeroProbe checks the real files, so
links and other spellings of the same path are caught), and
\f[V]-oA -\f[R] is refused.
All report files are opened (created, not truncated) before the scan
starts, so an unwritable path fails at once, as in nmap.
A file\[aq]s old content is replaced when the first part of the new
report is written, just before the first host group is scanned, so a
command that fails before scanning leaves the previous report in place.
The normal, XML, grepable and \f[V]-oS\f[R] reports are then written a
host group at a time as the scan goes (see \f[V]--max-hostgroup\f[R]),
so an aborted scan leaves reports of the hosts it finished, which
\f[V]--resume\f[R] can carry on from.
The JSON report is a single document and is written when the scan ends.
A \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] writes no reports and creates no files.
.PP
Report files are written through the handles opened at the start, so the
paths are not looked up again: replacing a report path with a link while
the scan runs does not redirect the report.
.PP
Under \f[V]sudo\f[R], FeroProbe has already given up root when it opens
the report files (see Giving up root under sudo), so it writes them as
the user who ran \f[V]sudo\f[R]: they belong to that user, and a path
that user cannot write to fails as it would for them.
When it does run as root (\f[V]--keep-root\f[R], or root without
\f[V]sudo\f[R]), it refuses to follow a symbolic link anywhere in a
report\[aq]s path, for a directory or the file itself, unless root owns
both the link and the directory holding it and no other user can write
to that directory (so \f[V]/dev/stdout\f[R] works, but
\f[V]sudo feroprobe --keep-root -oN /tmp/scan.txt\f[R] is refused when
\f[V]/tmp/scan.txt\f[R] is a link, whoever made it).
The path is opened one component at a time with \f[V]O_NOFOLLOW\f[R],
and an allowed link\[aq]s target is walked the same way, so a link it
leads through is checked too and a link swapped in at the last moment is
refused, and a file root creates under \f[V]sudo --keep-root\f[R] is
handed to the invoking user.
nmap writes its reports as root through any link.
.SS \f[V]--append-output\f[R]
.PP
Add the reports to the end of the \f[V]-oN\f[R], \f[V]-oX\f[R],
\f[V]-oS\f[R], \f[V]-oG\f[R], \f[V]-oJ\f[R] and \f[V]-oA\f[R] files
instead of replacing them, as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]--append-output\f[R] does.
Each run adds a complete report, so a normal or grepable file collects
one report after another.
An XML file then holds several \f[V]<nmaprun>\f[R] documents one after
another, each with its own \f[V]<?xml ...?>\f[R] header, which XML
parsers refuse as one document until it is split, just as with nmap; a
JSON file likewise holds one JSON document per run.
Reports written to standard output (\f[V]-\f[R]) are not affected.
.SS \f[V]--stylesheet <path or URL>\f[R]
.PP
Link this XSL stylesheet from the XML report, through an
\f[V]<?xml-stylesheet href=\[dq]...\[dq] type=\[dq]text/xsl\[dq]?>\f[R]
line after the \f[V]<!DOCTYPE>\f[R] line, as nmap does.
A browser that opens the report applies the stylesheet and shows the
report as a web page.
The value is written as given, so give a URL or a path that is valid
where the report will be opened.
.PP
Without \f[V]--stylesheet\f[R], \f[V]--webxml\f[R] or
\f[V]--no-stylesheet\f[R], the report links FeroProbe\[aq]s own
stylesheet, \f[V]feroprobe.xsl\f[R]: the copy installed with FeroProbe
as a \f[V]file://\f[R] URL (next to the binary, as in the release
archives, then \f[V]../share/feroprobe/\f[R] from the binary, then
\f[V]/usr/local/share/feroprobe/\f[R] and
\f[V]/usr/share/feroprobe/\f[R]), or the bare name
\f[V]feroprobe.xsl\f[R] when no copy is found, as nmap does with
\f[V]nmap.xsl\f[R].
The stylesheet is \f[V]docs/feroprobe.xsl\f[R] in the source; it is
written for FeroProbe (nmap\[aq]s \f[V]nmap.xsl\f[R] is not reused) and
also shows nmap\[aq]s own XML reports.
Most browsers refuse to apply a stylesheet to a local file or from
another site, so the reliable way to get a web page is to convert the
report:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
xsltproc -o report.html /usr/share/feroprobe/feroprobe.xsl report.xml
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Of \f[V]--stylesheet\f[R], \f[V]--webxml\f[R] and
\f[V]--no-stylesheet\f[R], the last one given wins, as in nmap.
.SS \f[V]--webxml\f[R]
.PP
Link the published copy of FeroProbe\[aq]s stylesheet,
\f[V]https://jkobierczynski.github.io/feroprobe/feroprobe.xsl\f[R],
instead of the installed one, so the link works on machines where
FeroProbe is not installed.
Short for \f[V]--stylesheet\f[R] with that URL.
.SS \f[V]--no-stylesheet\f[R]
.PP
Leave the \f[V]xml-stylesheet\f[R] line out of the XML report.
.SS \f[V]--resume <filename>\f[R]
.PP
Carry on with a scan that was aborted (with Ctrl-C, a lost connection, a
reboot), given its normal (\f[V]-oN\f[R]), XML (\f[V]-oX\f[R]) or
grepable (\f[V]-oG\f[R]) report; for \f[V]-oA\f[R], any of the three
files.
As in nmap, it takes no other options, except \f[V]--keep-root\f[R],
\f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R] and
\f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R].
Under \f[V]sudo\f[R], root is given up before the report is read, so a
scan whose report or files only root can reach is resumed with
\f[V]sudo feroprobe --keep-root --resume FILE\f[R] (a
\f[V]--keep-root\f[R] recorded in the report has no effect on this).
The two authorizations are explained below:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe --resume scan.gnmap
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The command line is read back from the report, so the resumed scan runs
with the same options, targets and report files.
Every target up to and including the last host the report lists is
skipped, and the scan continues with the next one, appending to the
existing reports (the command line is run with \f[V]--append-output\f[R]
added, as nmap does): the normal and grepable files get a second
\f[V]scan initiated\f[R] line, and the XML document is continued rather
than started again, so that it ends up complete.
Like nmap, the file is searched for grepable \f[V]Host:\f[R] lines
first, then XML \f[V]<address>\f[R] elements, then normal
\f[V]Scan report for\f[R] lines.
.PP
Because the reports are written a host group at a time, hosts of the
group that was running when the scan stopped are scanned again.
Hosts found down are not listed in the reports, so those after the last
listed host are probed again too.
Other things to know:
.IP \[bu] 2
If the report lists no finished host, the scan starts from the
beginning, with a warning.
.IP \[bu] 2
If the last host it lists is not among the targets (the targets were
given by a name that now resolves differently, for example), nothing is
left to scan, as in nmap, with a warning.
.IP \[bu] 2
With \f[V]--randomize-hosts\f[R] the targets come in a different order
each time, so some hosts may be scanned twice and others missed; a
warning says so, as in nmap.
.IP \[bu] 2
The JSON report (\f[V]-oJ\f[R]) is written only when a scan ends, so the
aborted run left none, and the one written after resuming lists only the
hosts scanned since; a warning says so.
.IP \[bu] 2
The settings files and ICS protection apply to the resumed scan as they
would to the command typed again.
.PP
A report is a plain text file that anyone can write, and resuming one
runs the command line it records: its targets, its options and its
report files.
Treat a report you did not write yourself like a script someone handed
you; FeroProbe prints the command line it resumes on standard error
before anything else happens.
Three safeguards, all differences from nmap:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Only the scan\[aq]s own reports are appended to.\f[R] Every report
file the recorded command line names (except \f[V]-\f[R] and
\f[V]/dev/null\f[R], \f[V]/dev/stdout\f[R], \f[V]/dev/stderr\f[R]) must
already exist and hold the report of that same scan: for \f[V]-oN\f[R],
\f[V]-oG\f[R] and \f[V]-oX\f[R], a \f[V]scan initiated ... as:\f[R] line
recording the same command line; for \f[V]-oS\f[R], the mangled
\f[V]Starting FeroProbe\f[R] line; for \f[V]-oJ\f[R], nothing yet or a
FeroProbe JSON document.
Otherwise the resume is refused before anything is sent, and a missing
report file is not created.
nmap appends to whatever the recorded command line names, so a forged
report can make it add text to any file the user can write (as root, any
file), text the scanned hosts partly choose (a service\[aq]s version
string, for example).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Authorizations are given again.\f[R] If the recorded command line
has \f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R] or
\f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R], the resume is refused unless the
same option is given beside \f[V]--resume\f[R]
(\f[V]feroprobe --allow-public-evasion --resume scan.nmap\f[R]).
A deliberate opt-in is not taken from a file.
Given beside \f[V]--resume\f[R], either option applies to the resumed
scan, as it would to the command typed again.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Root is not kept for it.\f[R] Under \f[V]sudo\f[R], the resumed
scan runs as the user who ran \f[V]sudo\f[R] like any other (see Giving
up root under sudo); with \f[V]--keep-root\f[R] or a root shell, the
checks above still apply.
.PP
Files the command line reads (\f[V]-iL\f[R], \f[V]--excludefile\f[R],
\f[V]--service-rules\f[R], \f[V]--oui-file\f[R]) are read as they would
be for the command typed again, so under \f[V]sudo\f[R] with root given
up, only files the user could read anyway.
.SS \f[V]--nmap-label\f[R]
.PP
Write \f[V]scanner=\[dq]nmap\[dq]\f[R] in the XML report instead of
\f[V]scanner=\[dq]feroprobe\[dq]\f[R], for importers that accept only
nmap\[aq]s reports.
A comment in the file still names FeroProbe.
.SS \f[V]--open\f[R]
.PP
Show only ports that are open or possibly open: \f[V]open\f[R],
\f[V]open|filtered\f[R] and \f[V]unfiltered\f[R], as in nmap.
No \f[V]Not shown\f[R] line is printed; a host with none of these shows
\f[V]No open ports found\f[R].
Applies to every output format.
.SS \f[V]--reason\f[R]
.PP
Add a REASON column with the reason each port got its state (see
Reasons).
XML and JSON always carry the reasons.
.SS \f[V]-v\f[R], \f[V]-vv\f[R]
.PP
Report progress on standard error: each phase as it starts and ends with
its time (\f[V]Initiating TCP SYN scan of 4 hosts\f[R],
\f[V]Completed TCP SYN scan in 1.21s\f[R]), how many hosts are up,
excluded hosts, and each open port as a scan finds it
(\f[V]Discovered open port 22/tcp on 10.0.0.5\f[R]).
\f[V]-vv\f[R] adds more notes, such as resolver hints.
Each \f[V]-v\f[R] also raises the Not shown limit.
When a host loses so many probes that FeroProbe slows down for it,
\f[V]-v\f[R] says so, as nmap does
(\f[V]Increasing send delay for 10.0.0.5 from 0 to 5 due to 11 out of 30 dropped probes since last increase.\f[R]).
The verbosity can be changed while the scan runs; see Runtime
interaction.
.SS \f[V]-d\f[R], \f[V]-dd\f[R], \f[V]-d<level>\f[R], \f[V]--debug[=<level>]\f[R]
.PP
Raise the debugging level, as nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-d\f[R] does: each
\f[V]-d\f[R] (or each extra \f[V]d\f[R], as in \f[V]-ddd\f[R]) raises
the debugging level and the verbosity by one; \f[V]-d<level>\f[R] (or
\f[V]--debug=<level>\f[R]) sets both to \f[V]<level>\f[R], from 0 to 10.
Options apply in command-line order, so \f[V]-v -d\f[R] gives verbosity
2 and debugging 1, while \f[V]-vv -d1\f[R] gives 1 and 1.
The level must be attached: \f[V]-d 3\f[R] is \f[V]-d\f[R] followed by
the target \f[V]3\f[R].
Like nmap\[aq]s, \f[V]-d\f[R] also turns on \f[V]--reason\f[R].
The output goes to standard error, with the \f[V]-v\f[R] progress:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-d\f[R]: a timing report before the scan
(\f[V]--------------- Timing report ---------------\f[R], with the host
group size, the RTT timeouts in milliseconds, \f[V]--max-scan-delay\f[R]
for TCP, UDP and SCTP, \f[V]--min-parallelism\f[R] and the parallelism
cap, \f[V]--max-retries\f[R], \f[V]--host-timeout\f[R],
\f[V]--min-rate\f[R] and \f[V]--max-rate\f[R]; 0 means not set),
\f[V]Increased max_successful_tryno for <host> to <n> (packet drop)\f[R]
each time a host answers only a resent probe, which lets its probes be
sent once more, and after the port scan
\f[V]Final times for host <address>: srtt: <us> rttvar: <us>  to: <us>\f[R]
with the host\[aq]s smoothed round-trip time, its variance and its probe
timeout in microseconds.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-d2\f[R]:
\f[V]Discovered <state> port <port>/<protocol> on <host>\f[R] for ports
in every state, not only open ones.
.IP \[bu] 2
With \f[V]--iflist\f[R], the reason a table could not be read.
.PP
nmap prints much more at high debugging levels (its scan engine\[aq]s
internal state); FeroProbe prints the messages above and adds more where
they help.
.SS \f[V]--stats-every <time>\f[R]
.PP
Print a progress line to standard error at this interval, for example
\f[V]Stats: 0:01:05 elapsed; TCP SYN scan: 45.20% done (4520 of 10000 probes)\f[R].
\f[V]0\f[R] turns it off.
Pressing a key during the scan prints the same line once; see Runtime
interaction.
.SS \f[V]--iflist\f[R]
.PP
Print the host\[aq]s network interfaces and routes, then stop, in
nmap\[aq]s layout.
The interface table has one row per address of each interface (an
interface without addresses shows \f[V](none)/0\f[R]), with its type
(\f[V]ethernet\f[R], \f[V]loopback\f[R], \f[V]point2point\f[R] or
\f[V]other\f[R]), whether it is up, its MTU and, for Ethernet, its MAC
address.
The route table lists each route that goes through a listed interface,
IPv4 first, then the longest prefixes first, with its metric and
gateway.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
************************INTERFACES************************
DEV  (SHORT) IP/MASK        TYPE     UP MTU   MAC
lo   (lo)    127.0.0.1/8    loopback up 65536
eth0 (eth0)  192.168.0.5/24 ethernet up 1500  00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E
eth0 (eth0)  fe80::5/64     ethernet up 1500  00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E

**************************ROUTES**************************
DST/MASK       DEV  METRIC GATEWAY
192.168.0.0/24 eth0 100
0.0.0.0/0      eth0 100    192.168.0.1
fe80::/64      eth0 256
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Needs no root and no target.
The routes are read on Linux only for now; elsewhere the route table
shows \f[V]ROUTES: NONE FOUND(!)\f[R] (with \f[V]-d\f[R], the reason).
On Windows neither table is available yet.
.SS \f[V]--noninteractive\f[R]
.PP
Do not read keys from the terminal during the scan; see Runtime
interaction.
.SS Runtime interaction
.PP
While a scan runs in a terminal, FeroProbe reads single keys from it, as
nmap does:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Key
T}@T{
Effect
T}
_
T{
\f[V]v\f[R] / \f[V]V\f[R]
T}@T{
Raise / lower the verbosity by one (\f[V]Verbosity Increased to 2.\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[V]d\f[R] / \f[V]D\f[R]
T}@T{
Raise / lower the debugging level by one
(\f[V]Debugging Increased to 1.\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[V]p\f[R] / \f[V]P\f[R]
T}@T{
Turn the packet trace on / off (\f[V]Packet Tracing enabled.\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[V]?\f[R]
T}@T{
List these keys
T}
T{
any other key
T}@T{
Print a status line, as \f[V]--stats-every\f[R] does
T}
.TE
.PP
The keys take effect at once: a higher verbosity also lists more ports
in the final report.
Their messages go to standard error, like the rest of the progress
output (nmap writes them to standard output).
Keys are read from the controlling terminal (\f[V]/dev/tty\f[R]), not
standard input, so they work with \f[V]-iL -\f[R]; only while FeroProbe
is the terminal\[aq]s foreground job, so a scan started in the
background with \f[V]&\f[R] reads nothing; and only during the scan
itself, not during \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] or after the scan.
Line editing and echo are turned off on the terminal while keys are read
and are restored when the scan ends, when FeroProbe exits, and on
Ctrl-C.
Keys are not read when FeroProbe was started with SIGTTOU ignored or
blocked: the terminal could then not keep a scan that was moved to the
background from changing another job\[aq]s settings.
\f[V]--noninteractive\f[R] turns key reading off.
Not available on Windows yet.
.SS Comparing two scans
.PP
\f[V]feroprobe-diff FILE1 FILE2\f[R] compares two XML reports
(\f[V]-oX\f[R]) and shows what changed between them: hosts that came up
or went away, ports that opened or closed, and changed services,
versions, OS matches and script output.
It is FeroProbe\[aq]s counterpart of nmap\[aq]s \f[V]ndiff\f[R], with
the same options (\f[V]-v\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R], \f[V]--xml\f[R]),
output and exit status (0 same, 1 different, 2 error), and reads
nmap\[aq]s reports too.
It is a separate program, installed next to \f[V]feroprobe\f[R]; see
feroprobe-diff(1) or docs/feroprobe-diff.md.
.SH CHECKING A SCAN BEFORE AND WHILE IT RUNS
.PP
These options show what FeroProbe will do or is doing.
\f[V]--explain\f[R] and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] are FeroProbe\[aq]s own;
nmap has nothing like them.
.SS \f[V]--explain\f[R]
.PP
Before anything is sent, print the plan made from the command line to
standard error, step by step, with the reason for each choice: the
targets, which hosts get ARP and which get ping probes (and which
probes), whether names are looked up, which scan type was picked and
why, where the ports came from, the timing settings, and what runs after
the port scan.
The scan then runs as usual.
Combined with \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] it explains the plan and lists the
probes without sending anything.
.SS \f[V]--packet-trace\f[R]
.PP
Print every probe sent and every answer received on standard output, as
nmap does: \f[V]SENT\f[R] for packets sent, \f[V]RCVD\f[R] for packets
received, and \f[V]CONN\f[R] for connect attempts and their result.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
SENT (0.0005s) ICMP [10.0.0.2 > 10.0.0.5 Echo request (type=8/code=0) id=7996 seq=0]
RCVD (0.0007s) ICMP [10.0.0.5 > 10.0.0.2 Echo reply (type=0/code=0) id=7996 seq=0] ttl=64
CONN (0.0006s) TCP localhost > 10.0.0.5:443 => Connection refused
SENT (0.0543s) TCP 10.0.0.2:48100 > 10.0.0.5:22 S seq=2448842408 win=65109
RCVD (0.0545s) TCP 10.0.0.5:22 > 10.0.0.2:48100 SA seq=1290442 win=64240 ttl=64
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
With \f[V]-f\f[R]/\f[V]--mtu\f[R] every fragment gets its own line (see
Firewall/IDS evasion and spoofing).
As in nmap, \f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] also turns on the script trace.
.SS \f[V]--dry-run\f[R]
.PP
Send nothing.
Print the host-discovery and port-scan probes the scan would send, one
line per probe in the \f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] layout with the prefix
\f[V]NOT SENT (dry run)\f[R], then stop.
It covers host discovery and every port scan, with
\f[V]-f\f[R]/\f[V]--mtu\f[R], \f[V]-D\f[R] and \f[V]-S\f[R] applied, so
the lines show the real flags, sequence numbers, window, payload sizes,
fragments and source addresses.
Comment lines starting with \f[V]#\f[R] group the probes by target and
phase and end with a count.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Dry run (--dry-run): these are the probes the scan would send. Nothing is sent.
# Target 10.0.0.5 from 10.0.0.2
# Host discovery
NOT SENT (dry run) ICMP [10.0.0.2 > 10.0.0.5 Echo request (type=8/code=0) id=14847 seq=0]
\&...
# TCP scan with raw packets, flags S
NOT SENT (dry run) TCP 10.0.0.2:43970 > 10.0.0.5:22 S seq=1654920040 win=65109
# Dry run done: 4 discovery and 1 port-scan probe for 1 host listed, nothing sent; ...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Because nothing is sent, nothing comes back: there are no \f[V]RCVD\f[R]
lines and no port states.
A dry run therefore assumes every target is up and lists the port-scan
probes for all of them (a real run scans only the hosts discovery
finds), lists only the first attempt of each probe (a real run resends
unanswered ones as its hosts\[aq] losses call for, up to
\f[V]--retries\f[R] times), and only names the steps that depend on
answers (\f[V]-sV\f[R], \f[V]--traceroute\f[R], reverse DNS) instead of
listing their probes.
OS detection (\f[V]-O\f[R]) is listed probe by probe for one try, with
\f[V]<open>\f[R] and \f[V]<closed>\f[R] in place of the ports the scan
would find, and a count of its own in the last line.
When the scan covers a single TCP port it cannot find both an open and a
closed one, so the \f[V]<closed>\f[R] probes are left out (and with
\f[V]--osscan-limit\f[R] no OS probes are listed at all).
Target hostnames are still resolved through the system resolver, even
with \f[V]-n\f[R] (give numeric addresses when no DNS traffic is
allowed), so the addresses shown are the real ones, and the routing
table is consulted (without sending) for the source address.
No reverse DNS query is made.
With \f[V]--traceroute\f[R], a note names the TCP port traceroute would
fall back to (or says hosts would not be traced).
With \f[V]-sV\f[R], the notes describe its probes instead of listing
them, since they go only to the TCP ports found open and the UDP ports
found \f[V]open\f[R] or \f[V]open|filtered\f[R]: at the intensity in
use, the TLS-first ports, the probes meant for each scanned port, the
listen probe, then the other probes in the order they would be tried,
for TCP and UDP.
With \f[V]--script\f[R], the scripts are loaded (so a bad one fails the
dry run) and a note names each with its rules and the scope its sockets
would have; what scripts send is not listed, since their rules depend on
what the scan finds and what they send on the replies they get.
.PP
\f[B]A dry run is not a preview of nmap.\f[R] It lists FeroProbe\[aq]s
probes.
nmap run with the same options sends different packets, and some of its
features probe outside \f[V]-p\f[R], so a dry run must not be used to
check an nmap command before running it.
What nmap sends in addition or instead, per option:
.PP
.TS
