socket exists), all output options, \f[V]--explain\f[R],
\f[V]--dry-run\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
Instead of running as root on Linux, you can grant the binary just the
capability it needs:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep /path/to/feroprobe
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Without raw sockets, a scan with no \f[V]-s\f[R] option runs a connect
scan, and discovery uses its unprivileged probes; an explicit raw scan
type, \f[V]-O\f[R] or \f[V]--traceroute\f[R] is an error that says what
is needed.
\f[V]-A\f[R] degrades to \f[V]-sV\f[R] with a warning naming what it
skipped (nmap degrades silently).
.PP
Linux has every feature.
On macOS and Windows FeroProbe runs the connect and UDP scans, discovery
with its unprivileged probes, \f[V]-sV\f[R], and every output format;
the raw-socket features are Linux only.
.SS Giving up root under sudo
.PP
Only raw sockets need root.
So when root runs FeroProbe under \f[V]sudo\f[R], it reads its settings
files first and then becomes the user who ran \f[V]sudo\f[R]
(\f[V]SUDO_UID\f[R], \f[V]SUDO_GID\f[R] and that user\[aq]s groups)
before it opens any other file or starts a thread.
On Linux it keeps just \f[V]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R], \f[V]CAP_NET_ADMIN\f[R]
and \f[V]CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\f[R] (for \f[V]-g\f[R] below 1024), so
every raw-socket feature still works; on other systems, where raw scans
are not supported, it gives root up entirely.
Everything else in the run (service and banner parsing, TLS,
\f[V]-iL\f[R], \f[V]--excludefile\f[R], \f[V]--service-rules\f[R],
\f[V]--resume\f[R] and the report files) then happens as that user.
\f[V]-v\f[R] prints a line saying so.
.PP
This means a \f[V]sudo feroprobe\f[R] run reads and writes files with
your own permissions: \f[V]-iL /root/targets.txt\f[R] or
\f[V]-oN /root/scan.txt\f[R] fail unless you can reach them yourself.
\f[V]--keep-root\f[R] stays root for such a run.
Root without \f[V]sudo\f[R] (a root shell with no \f[V]SUDO_UID\f[R])
keeps root, as there is no other user to become.
.PP
nmap stays root for the whole run, which is why a sudoers entry that
allows only \f[V]nmap\f[R] is a well-known way to become root.
Do not rely on a restricted sudoers entry for FeroProbe either:
\f[V]--keep-root\f[R], and options that read files, still put root\[aq]s
privileges in the hands of whoever runs it.
Grant the capability with \f[V]setcap\f[R] instead (above), or give such
users full \f[V]sudo\f[R].
.SH PROBE SIGNATURE
.PP
FeroProbe\[aq]s probes carry a stable, documented signature so that
network owners can recognize them:
.IP \[bu] 2
Raw TCP scans (\f[V]-sS\f[R], \f[V]-sF\f[R], \f[V]-sN\f[R],
\f[V]-sX\f[R], \f[V]-sA\f[R], \f[V]-sW\f[R], \f[V]-sM\f[R],
\f[V]--scanflags\f[R]) set the TCP window to 65109 (\f[V]0xFE55\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
UDP probes to ports without a service payload carry
\f[V]FeroProbeScan/1\[rs]n\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
ICMP and ICMPv6 echo requests (host discovery) carry the 9-byte payload
\f[V]feroprobe\f[R], where nmap\[aq]s are empty.
.PP
A payload given with \f[V]--data\f[R], \f[V]--data-string\f[R] or
\f[V]--data-length\f[R] replaces the UDP and ICMP echo markers (the TCP
window mark stays), and the FTP bounce scan (\f[V]-b\f[R]) sends only
ordinary FTP commands to the relay.
.PP
The connect scan and OS detection probes carry no mark, by design.
Suricata and Snort rules matching the signature are in the
repository\[aq]s \f[V]rules/\f[R] directory.
Details are in docs/responsible-use.md.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM NMAP
.PP
FeroProbe follows nmap unless nmap\[aq]s behaviour is clearly wrong or a
safeguard calls for a difference.
The deliberate differences are:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Behaviour
T}@T{
nmap
T}@T{
FeroProbe
T}
_
T{
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] with \f[V]-f\f[R]/\f[V]--mtu\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints one \f[V]SENT\f[R] line for the unfragmented probe, although
fragments are what goes on the wire
T}@T{
Prints one line per fragment actually sent.
nmap\[aq]s display is treated as a bug
T}
T{
Evasion options (\f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--mtu\f[R], \f[V]-D\f[R],
\f[V]-S\f[R])
T}@T{
Allowed against any address
T}@T{
Raw IPv4 TCP scans only; public targets, decoys or spoofed sources need
\f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R]
T}
T{
Decoy order (\f[V]-D ...,ME,...\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[V]ME\f[R] places the real probe
T}@T{
\f[V]ME\f[R] is accepted, but the real probe always goes last
T}
T{
Probe signature
T}@T{
None (empty ICMP echo payload)
T}@T{
Raw TCP probes use window 65109; generic UDP probes carry
\f[V]FeroProbeScan/1\[rs]n\f[R]; ICMP echo requests carry
\f[V]feroprobe\f[R].
A \f[V]--data\f[R], \f[V]--data-string\f[R] or \f[V]--data-length\f[R]
payload replaces the UDP and echo markers, as nmap\[aq]s replaces its
payloads
T}
T{
FTP bounce scan (\f[V]-b\f[R])
T}@T{
Allowed against any target through any relay
T}@T{
Public targets or a public relay need \f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]--spoof-mac\f[R]
T}@T{
Allowed against any address; IPv6 frames spoofed too; \f[V]0\f[R] may
pick a multicast address
T}@T{
Public targets need \f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R]; IPv4 and ARP only
(IPv6 probes keep the real address, with a warning); \f[V]0\f[R] always
picks a unicast address
T}
T{
\f[V]--badsum\f[R] with \f[V]-O\f[R]
T}@T{
Also spoils the OS-detection probes (nmap\[aq]s checksum routine applies
it to every packet), which makes their answers meaningless
T}@T{
OS-detection probes keep correct checksums
T}
T{
Unprivileged discovery
T}@T{
TCP connects to 80 and 443 only
T}@T{
The same, plus an ICMP echo through an unprivileged ping socket where
the OS offers one
T}
T{
\f[V]--explain\f[R], \f[V]--dry-run\f[R]
T}@T{
No equivalent
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own planning and checking options
T}
T{
Settings files, \f[V]ics_protection\f[R],
\f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R]
T}@T{
No equivalent
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own; off by default, so default behaviour is unchanged.
A \f[V].feroproberc\f[R] in the working directory can switch protection
on but not off
T}
T{
JSON output (\f[V]-oJ\f[R])
T}@T{
No equivalent
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own format
T}
T{
XML \f[V]scanner\f[R] attribute
T}@T{
\f[V]nmap\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]feroprobe\f[R], unless \f[V]--nmap-label\f[R]
T}
T{
XML stylesheet
T}@T{
Links \f[V]nmap.xsl\f[R]; \f[V]--webxml\f[R] links nmap.org\[aq]s copy
T}@T{
Links FeroProbe\[aq]s own \f[V]feroprobe.xsl\f[R]; \f[V]--webxml\f[R]
links FeroProbe\[aq]s published copy
T}
T{
Privileges under \f[V]sudo\f[R]
T}@T{
Stays root for the whole run
T}@T{
Becomes the user who ran \f[V]sudo\f[R] once the settings files are
read, keeping only the network capabilities on Linux, so reports,
\f[V]-iL\f[R] and other files are read and written as that user;
\f[V]--keep-root\f[R] stays root (see Giving up root under sudo)
T}
T{
Report files as root
T}@T{
Written through any symbolic link
T}@T{
With \f[V]--keep-root\f[R] or without \f[V]sudo\f[R]: a link is refused
unless root owns it and its directory and no other user can write there
(\f[V]/dev/stdout\f[R] works); a new file under \f[V]sudo\f[R] belongs
to the invoking user (see \f[V]-oA\f[R] under Output options)
T}
T{
Service and OS signatures
T}@T{
nmap\[aq]s databases
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own (nmap\[aq]s cannot be redistributed); the OS
database is much smaller than nmap\[aq]s, so fewer hosts get a perfect
match
T}
T{
UDP service payloads (\f[V]-sU\f[R])
T}@T{
nmap\[aq]s payload database: several probes per port (two DNS queries to
53, two NTP versions to 123, SNMPv1 and v3 to 161, three NetBIOS queries
to 137), up to dozens with \f[V]-sV\f[R]
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own, one per port: DNS (53, 5353), NetBIOS NODE STATUS
(137, transaction ID \f[V]0x4650\f[R] \[dq]FP\[dq], the request
\f[V]nbstat\f[R] sends), NTP (123), SNMP (161) and SSDP (1900); every
other port gets \f[V]FeroProbeScan/1\[rs]n\f[R].
nmap\[aq]s payloads cannot be redistributed; one payload per service is
enough to make it answer
T}
T{
OS detection probes (\f[V]-O\f[R])
T}@T{
About 16 probes per try (6 SYNs with varied options, ECN, T2-T7 to open
and closed ports, 2 ICMP echoes, a UDP probe to a closed port), with
closed ports picked at random outside the scan when none was found, and
hosts without an open port probed too
T}@T{
At most 9 probes per try (4 SYNs, ECN and an ACK to the open port; a SYN
and an ACK to a closed port the scan found; an ICMP echo only if
discovery sends echo requests).
No UDP probe, no T3/T5-T7-style odd flag combinations, no random ports,
and hosts without an open TCP port are not probed.
The price: fewer tests, so some systems nmap tells apart look alike
T}
T{
OS uptime and TCP sequence lines (\f[V]-O -v\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[V]Uptime guess\f[R] from the TCP timestamps,
\f[V]TCP Sequence Prediction\f[R] difficulty
T}@T{
Not printed.
Most current systems give each connection its own random timestamp
offset, so the uptime nmap prints for them is wrong; sequence
predictability is left out with it
T}
T{
Port names
T}@T{
nmap-services
T}@T{
The same names for the common ports, from the IANA registry, and
\f[V]unknown\f[R] for the rest.
nmap-services cannot be redistributed
T}
T{
Timeouts
T}@T{
Adapt to measured round-trip times
T}@T{
The same (nmap\[aq]s formula, limits and template values);
\f[V]--timeout\f[R] fixes them, an option nmap does not have
T}
T{
Congestion control, \f[V]--max-retries\f[R], \f[V]--max-scan-delay\f[R]
T}@T{
Windows per host and group between \f[V]--min-parallelism\f[R] and
\f[V]--max-parallelism\f[R]; resends as losses show up, up to
\f[V]--max-retries\f[R]; raises a lossy host\[aq]s scan delay, saying so
with \f[V]-v\f[R]
T}@T{
The same, except: a connect or UDP probe that already gave up is not
sent again when its host\[aq]s resends go up later; no timing pings to
detect losses; the raised delays are not reported
T}
T{
\f[V]-g\f[R] with UDP scans
T}@T{
Raw UDP packets from the port, any port including 0
T}@T{
UDP probes go through the system\[aq]s sockets, which share the port
between them; port 0 cannot be used and falls back to a random port,
with a warning
T}
T{
Old-style IPv4 numbers as targets (\f[V]2222\f[R], \f[V]10.1.2\f[R],
\f[V]010.0.0.1\f[R])
T}@T{
Passed to the resolver
T}@T{
Refused with an explanation
T}
T{
Probes outside the requested ports
T}@T{
\f[V]-A\f[R]\[aq]s scripts can probe other ports (\f[V]ms-sql-info\f[R]:
UDP 1434 on every host); \f[V]-O\f[R] picks a random UDP port (and for
IPv4 also TCP), regardless of \f[V]-p\f[R], when the scan found no
closed or unfiltered one
T}@T{
Never beyond the command\[aq]s own ports (\f[V]-p\f[R] plus the
host-discovery ports): \f[V]-sV\f[R] probes only open ports the scan
found, \f[V]-O\f[R] an open TCP port the scan found and, when it found
one, a closed one (and ICMP echo only when discovery sends it),
traceroute only a scanned port, a discovery port, or (with
\f[V]-sn\f[R]) the \f[V]-p\f[R] port, and scripts only the ports the
scan probed on the host they run against
T}
T{
Traceroute port
T}@T{
The probe that got the best answer during discovery or the scan, else
ICMP echo
T}@T{
TCP SYNs to an open or closed scanned port, else a TCP port the command
names (\f[V]-p\f[R] with \f[V]-sn\f[R], or a discovery port), else the
host is not traced (FeroProbe used to fall back to port 80)
T}
T{
\f[V]-A\f[R] without root
T}@T{
Silently drops \f[V]-O\f[R] and traceroute
T}@T{
Drops them with a warning naming them
T}
T{
\f[V]-sL\f[R] with \f[V]-P\f[R] options
T}@T{
Ignores them silently
T}@T{
Ignores them with a warning per option
T}
T{
\f[V]-sL\f[R] with \f[V]-sV\f[R] or \f[V]--traceroute\f[R]
T}@T{
Silently drops them (and as root still looks up routes, so local targets
can get ARP requests)
T}@T{
Refuses the combination; a list scan sends the targets nothing at all
T}
T{
\f[V]-PR\f[R]
T}@T{
A no-op; says so only with \f[V]-v\f[R]
T}@T{
A no-op; says so on every run
T}
T{
Script library
T}@T{
nmap\[aq]s NSE libraries (\f[V]stdnse\f[R], \f[V]shortport\f[R],
\f[V]nmap.new_socket\f[R], ...)
and its own scripts
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own \f[V]fero\f[R] library and scripts (nmap\[aq]s
cannot be shipped under their licence); nmap\[aq]s scripts do not run
unchanged
T}
T{
Script sockets
T}@T{
A script may connect anywhere; prerule scripts may broadcast
T}@T{
Only to the host the script runs against, on ports the scan probed
there; prerule and postrule scripts get no network access.
A refused connection prints a warning
T}
T{
Script sandbox
T}@T{
Scripts may read and write files and run commands (\f[V]io\f[R],
\f[V]os\f[R])
T}@T{
No files, commands or environment; memory and instruction limits per run
T}
T{
Script output text
T}@T{
Every byte outside printable ASCII shown as \f[V]\[rs]xHH\f[R]
T}@T{
UTF-8 text kept; control characters and invalid bytes shown as
\f[V]\[rs]xHH\f[R]
T}
T{
Script table output
T}@T{
Named entries in Lua\[aq]s traversal order (unpredictable) unless the
script orders them
T}@T{
Named entries sorted by name
T}
T{
Script output on folded ports
T}@T{
Not shown when the port\[aq]s state is folded into \f[V]Not shown\f[R]
T}@T{
The port is listed with its output
T}
T{
\f[V]fero.args.get\f[R] of a bare flag
T}@T{
\f[V]stdnse.get_script_args\f[R] returns \f[V]1\f[R]
T}@T{
Returns \f[V]true\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[V]--script-help\f[R]
T}@T{
Prints a link to nmap.org\[aq]s documentation of each script, and writes
\f[V]-oX\f[R] XML
T}@T{
Prints where the script came from (built in, or its file); no XML
T}
T{
Default scripts (\f[V]-sC\f[R])
T}@T{
nmap\[aq]s own set of over a hundred scripts, some reaching other ports
(\f[V]ms-sql-info\f[R]: UDP 1434) or outside hosts
(\f[V]http-open-proxy\f[R])
T}@T{
FeroProbe\[aq]s own, smaller set (see Built-in scripts), within the
scan\[aq]s scope.
\f[V]ftp-anon\f[R] lists no files, \f[V]sshv1\f[R] reads the greeting
only, \f[V]smtp-commands\f[R] sends no HELP, \f[V]ssh-hostkey\f[R] has
no \f[V]visual\f[R] or \f[V]bubble\f[R] form and offers no ECDH key
exchange on NIST curves
T}
T{
Script traffic signature
T}@T{
\f[V]SSH-2.0-Nmap-SSH2-Hostkey\f[R]; EHLO with the target\[aq]s own
name, else \f[V]nmap.scanme.org\f[R]; FTP password \f[V]IEUser\[at]\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[V]SSH-2.0-FeroProbe_Scripting_Engine\f[R],
\f[V]EHLO feroprobe.invalid\f[R], FTP password \f[V]feroprobe\[at]\f[R]
(see \f[V]docs/responsible-use.md\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[V]--resume\f[R]
T}@T{
Runs the recorded command line as it is, creating or appending to any
report file it names
T}@T{
Prints the command line first; appends only to existing files that hold
that scan\[aq]s report (creates none); a recorded
\f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R] or \f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R]
must be given again beside \f[V]--resume\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
Behaviour that looks unusual but matches nmap includes: skipping
discovery for this machine as root (\f[V]localhost-response\f[R]), ARP
or neighbour discovery deciding local hosts alone, the default discovery
probes, the 25-port \f[V]Not shown\f[R] rule, \f[V]--exclude-ports\f[R]
thinning the ping probes, shuffled port order, and skipping (rather than
failing) a scan type left without ports.
.SH NMAP OPTIONS FEROPROBE DOES NOT HAVE
.PP
These nmap options are refused as unknown:
.IP \[bu] 2
Scans and discovery: \f[V]-sI\f[R] (idle scan, not planned),
\f[V]-iR\f[R], \f[V]--resolve-all\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Output and misc: \f[V]--port-ratio\f[R].
.PP
DEVELOPMENT.md tracks which of these are planned.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Status
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
0
T}@T{
The scan (or dry run) completed.
Hosts being down or ports closed is not an error
T}
T{
1
T}@T{
FeroProbe refused the command or the scan failed; the reason is printed
to standard error after \f[V]Error:\f[R]
T}
T{
2
T}@T{
The command line could not be parsed (an unknown option or an invalid
value)
T}
.TE
.PP
Warnings, progress (\f[V]-v\f[R], \f[V]--stats-every\f[R]) and
\f[V]--explain\f[R] go to standard error; reports,
\f[V]--packet-trace\f[R] and \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] output go to standard
output.
.SH FILES
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Path
T}@T{
Use
T}
_
T{
\f[V]/usr/share/hwdata/oui.txt\f[R],
\f[V]/usr/share/ieee-data/oui.txt\f[R] and similar
T}@T{
MAC vendor names, unless \f[V]--oui-file\f[R] is given
T}
T{
\f[V]\[ti]/.config/feroprobe/config.toml\f[R]
(\f[V]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]), the invoking user\[aq]s under
\f[V]sudo\f[R], and \f[V]./.feroproberc\f[R]
T}@T{
Settings; see Settings files and ICS protection
T}
T{
The system resolver\[aq]s configuration (\f[V]/etc/resolv.conf\f[R] on
Unix)
T}@T{
Resolving target hostnames, and reverse DNS without
\f[V]--dns-servers\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
FeroProbe needs no other data files: its port rankings, service rules,
OS signatures and scripts are built into the binary.
Scripts of your own are read from the files and directories
\f[V]--script\f[R] names.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Discover hosts on a network, then scan their 1000 most common TCP ports
feroprobe 192.168.1.0/24

# Ping sweep only
sudo feroprobe -sn 192.168.1.0/24

# Every TCP port of one host, with the reason for each state
sudo feroprobe -sS -p- --reason 10.0.0.5

# TCP and UDP together, skipping discovery
feroprobe -Pn -sT -sU -p 53,123,161 scanme.example.org

# Services and versions, OS and route in one go
sudo feroprobe -A -p 22,80,443 10.0.0.5

# Custom discovery probes
sudo feroprobe -sn -PE -PP -PS22,443 -PA80 10.0.0.0/24

# SCTP signalling ports, and which IP protocols a host speaks
sudo feroprobe -sY -p S:2905,3868 10.0.0.5
sudo feroprobe -sO -p P:1,6,17,47,50,132 10.0.0.5

# IPv6: a local prefix, a link-local host, and the IPv6 address of a name
sudo feroprobe -sn 2001:db8::/120 fe80::1%eth0
feroprobe -6 -p 22,443 scanme.example.org

# Gentle scan of a fragile network: check the probes first, then run it
# (same privileges for both, since root changes the default scan type)
sudo feroprobe --dry-run -T1 --max-rate 10 -p 502,102 10.10.0.0/28
sudo feroprobe -T1 --max-rate 10 -p 502,102 10.10.0.0/28

# Test your own IDS with fragments and decoys (private range)
sudo feroprobe -sS -f -D 10.0.0.7,ME,10.0.0.8 -p 80 --packet-trace 10.0.0.5

# Read targets from a file, write all report formats
feroprobe -iL hosts.txt --exclude 10.0.0.1 -oA weekly

# Grep open ports from grepable output on standard output
feroprobe -oG - 192.168.1.0/24 | grep \[aq]/open/\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
